            D) It has no effect on speed — only the H-bridge direction inputs matter.
        -->

    </section>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <section xml:id="sec-motors-day11">
        <title>Day 11 In-Class: Motors, the H-Bridge, and PWM</title>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Today we work out what sets a motor's speed, and then build the hardware
                that controls it.  We will follow the actuator signal chain that carries a
                number out of the STM32C031C6 and turns it into motion, derive the
                relationship between the voltage at a motor's terminals and the speed of
                its shaft, see how an H-bridge reverses a motor and how the TB6612 driver
                exposes that through a few logic pins, and then wire a motor and drive it —
                first by hand, to set its direction, and then under a program that ramps its
                speed with PWM.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <!-- Part 1 -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day11-actuator-chain">
            <title>Part 1: The Actuator Chain, and What Sets the Speed</title>
            <p>
                In the ADC Chapter we drew the <term>sensor signal chain</term>
                (<xref ref="fig-adc-signal-chain"/>): a physical
                quantity enters from the world, a sensor turns it into a voltage, an
                analog front end conditions it, the ADC turns it into an integer, and the
                MCU computes with that integer.  Driving an actuator runs the same chain
                in reverse.  A number leaves the MCU, a converter turns it into a voltage,
                a driver raises that to the larger voltage and current the actuator needs,
                and the actuator turns it into a physical quantity — here, the rotation of
                a motor.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-actuator-chain">
                <caption>The actuator signal chain, the mirror of the sensor chain from
                the ADC chapter.  The MCU produces an integer command; a converter turns it into a
                voltage; a driver raises that to the voltage and current the actuator
                needs; and the actuator turns it into motion.  Because the STM32C031C6 has
                no true digital-to-analog converter, a PWM signal stands in for the
                converter — which is why this chapter builds both the driver and the
                PWM.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day11-Motors/actuator-signal-chain.svg" width="95%"/>
            </figure>
            <p>
                Two stages of that chain are new, and they are what this chapter is about:
                the <term>driver</term>, in our case we'll use the TB6612 motor driver IC, and
                the converter, which on our chip is a PWM signal rather than a true
                <term>DAC</term> (digital-to-analog converter, the counterpart of the ADC
                from Day 7).  In the reading you learned: a
                motor's direction is set by the polarity of its voltage, and its speed by
                the average value of that voltage.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-motor-circuit">
                <caption>The motor drawn as a circuit.  The supply voltage <m>V</m> drives a
                current <m>i</m> through the armature's resistance <m>R_a</m>, and the
                spinning motor itself acts as a generator producing the back-EMF <m>e</m>,
                which opposes the supply.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day11-Motors/motor-circuit.png" width="72%"/>
            </figure>

            <p>
                The reading said that a motor's steady speed follows the voltage across its
                terminals.  That is worth deriving, because the relationships behind it also
                tell us what <em>else</em> sets the speed.  Writing <m>\tau</m> for the
                torque, <m>i</m> for the current through the armature, <m>\omega</m> for the
                rotational speed, <m>e</m> for the back-EMF and <m>V</m> for the voltage at
                the terminals, four relationships describe the motor:
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li>The torque is proportional to the current, with a constant
                <m>K_t</m> that the motor's construction fixes:
                <m>\tau = K_t\, i</m>.</li>
                <li>At a steady speed the motor is no longer accelerating, so the torque is
                just overcoming friction: <m>\tau = b\, \omega</m>, where <m>b</m>
                describes the friction.</li>
                <li>The back-EMF is proportional to the speed, with its own constant
                <m>K_e</m>: <m>e = K_e\, \omega</m>.</li>
                <li>The armature coils have a resistance <m>R_a</m>, and the voltage left
                over after the back-EMF is what drives the current through it:
                <m>V - e = i\, R_a</m>.</li>
            </ul>
            <figure xml:id="fig-motor-relations">
                <caption>The four relationships together, in the symbols used above.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day11-Motors/motor-relations.svg" width="34%"/>
            </figure>

            <p>
                Combining those four equations to eliminate <m>i</m> and
                <m>e</m> yields a relationship that only has <m>V</m> and <m>\omega</m> in it: this is the speed the motor
                settles at:
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-equilibrium-speed">
                <caption>The speed a DC motor settles at for a given terminal voltage.
                Everything in the denominator is a constant of the motor and its load, so the
                steady speed is proportional to <m>V</m>.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day11-Motors/motor-equilibrium-speed.svg" width="52%"/>
            </figure>
            <p>
                Every symbol in the denominator is a constant of the motor and its load, so
                the speed is proportional to the terminal voltage — the fact the reading
                asked you to take on trust, and the reason that varying the average voltage
                is how we vary the speed.  Note what the equation does <em>not</em> cover:
                it balances a torque against friction at a steady speed, so it says nothing
                about what happens in the moment you switch a stopped motor on.  You will
                learn to model that in ENGS 22 or ENGS 26.
            </p>
            <slide xml:id="sl-day11-actuator-chain" ref="fig-actuator-chain">
    <caption>The sensor chain of the ADC chapter, run backwards: an integer leaves the MCU (microcontroller unit), a converter turns it into a voltage, a driver raises that to the voltage and current the actuator needs, and motion comes out. All but the smallest actuators need a driver. The two middle boxes are what we build today — the TB6612 driver, and a PWM signal standing in for the DAC (digital-to-analog converter), because the STM32C031C6 has no true one.</caption>
    <note>Open here. The chain is the map for the whole day: PWM stands in for the DAC, the TB6612 is the driver.</note>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day11-motor-relations" room="compressed">
    <sidebyside widths="52% 44%" margins="2%" valign="middle">
        <image source="images/Day11-Motors/motor-circuit.png"/>
        <ul>
          <li>τ – torque</li>
          <li>i – current</li>
          <li>ω – rotational speed</li>
          <li>e – back-EMF</li>
          <li>V – terminal voltage</li>
      </ul>
    </sidebyside>
    <p>Current → electromagnet field → repels permanent magnet field → torque.</p>
    <ul>
        <li>Torque is proportional to current: <m>\tau = K_t\, i</m>. </li>
        <li>At a steady speed, torque just overcomes friction: <m>\tau = b\, \omega</m>.</li>
        <li>A moving coil in a magnetic field generates a voltage, the back-EMF, and it is proportional to speed: <m>e = K_e\, \omega</m>.</li>
        <li>The motor coils have some resistance, and the voltage left over after the back-EMF is what drives the current through them: <m>V - e = i\, R_a</m>.</li>
    </ul>
    <caption>K_t – torque constant, b – friction, K_e – generator constant, R_a – armature resistance.</caption>
    <note>The circuit and the equations are the same story twice: point at the loop while you say each one.</note>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day11-equilibrium-speed">
    <p>Combining those four to eliminate the torque, the current and the back-EMF leaves a relationship between just the terminal voltage and the speed the motor settles at:</p>
    <image source="images/Day11-Motors/motor-equilibrium-speed.svg" width="25%"/>
    <ul>
        <li>Everything in the denominator is a constant of the motor and its load, so the steady speed is proportional to the voltage at the terminals. This is the reason that varying the average voltage is how we vary the speed.</li>
        <li>It balances a torque against friction at a steady speed, so it says nothing about the moment you switch a stopped motor on.</li>
    </ul>
</slide>

            <activity xml:id="act-day11-speed-params">
                <title>What else changes the speed?</title>
                <introduction>
                    <p>Work through both parts with this equation in front of you:</p>
                    <image source="images/Day11-Motors/motor-equilibrium-speed.svg" width="30%"/>
                </introduction>
                <task xml:id="task-day11-params-table">
                    <statement>
                        <p>Fill in the right-hand column of the table.  For each row, decide
                        first from physical intuition whether increasing that quantity should
                        make the motor turn faster or slower, and then check your answer
                        against the equation.  </p>
                        <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="major" left="minor" right="minor">
                            <col halign="left" width="55%"/>
                            <col halign="center" width="45%"/>
                            <row bottom="major">
                                <cell>Increase this quantity</cell>
                                <cell>Does <m>\omega</m> increase or decrease?</cell>
                            </row>
                            <row bottom="minor">
                                <cell>Friction, <m>b</m></cell><cell></cell>
                            </row>
                            <row bottom="minor">
                                <cell>Armature resistance, <m>R_a</m></cell><cell></cell>
                            </row>
                            <row bottom="minor">
                                <cell>Generator constant, <m>K_e</m>, where <m>e = K_e \omega</m></cell><cell></cell>
                            </row>
                            <row>
                                <cell>Torque constant, <m>K_t</m>, where <m>\tau = K_t i</m></cell><cell></cell>
                            </row>
                        </tabular>
                    </statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day11-params-startup">
                    <statement><p>Now the case the equation does not cover.  At the instant you
                    connect a stopped motor to a voltage <m>V</m>, the shaft is not yet turning.
                    What is the back-EMF at that instant, and what current flows through the
                    armature?  Compare that current with the current the same motor draws once
                    it has come up to speed.  Which is larger, and why?</p></statement>
                </task>
            </activity>
            <instructor xml:id="inst-day11-speed-params">
                <p>The table.  Increasing the friction <m>b</m> increases the denominator, so
                the speed <em>decreases</em> — more of the torque goes into fighting friction.
                Increasing the armature resistance <m>R_a</m> also increases the denominator,
                so the speed <em>decreases</em>: less current flows for the same voltage, so
                less torque.  Increasing the generator constant <m>K_e</m> increases the
                denominator too, so the speed <em>decreases</em> — a motor that generates more
                back-EMF per unit speed reaches the balance point sooner.  Increasing the
                torque constant <m>K_t</m> makes the term <m>b R_a / K_t</m> smaller, so the
                denominator shrinks and the speed <em>increases</em>: more torque per amp.</p>
                <p>The startup current.  At the instant of switch-on <m>\omega = 0</m>, so
                <m>e = K_e \omega = 0</m>: there is no back-EMF yet.  The full supply voltage
                then appears across the armature resistance alone, and
                <m>i = V / R_a</m> — the largest current the motor will ever draw.  Once the
                shaft is up to speed the back-EMF opposes the supply, only
                <m>V - e</m> is left across <m>R_a</m>, and the current is much smaller.  This
                is the current a motor driver has to survive, and it is why we will care
                how much current the driver can pass.</p>
            </instructor>
        </subsection>

        <!-- Part 2 -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day11-hbridge">
            <title>Part 2: The H-Bridge Reverses the Motor</title>
            <p>
                Reversing a motor means reversing the polarity of the voltage across it,
                and in an embedded system there is no hand to swap the two wires.  The
                <term>H-bridge</term> does it electronically.  As we saw in the reading
                (<xref ref="fig-hbridge-concept"/>), it is four transistor switches around the motor:
                two upper switches to the motor supply and two lower switches to ground.
                The upper two transistors are
                P-channel switches, which are on when their gate is LOW, and the lower two are
                N-channel switches, which are on when their gate is HIGH.
            </p>
            <slide xml:id="sl-day11-reverse-polarity" ref="fig-hbridge-concept">
    <ul>
        <li>To reverse the direction of the motor, switch the + and − connections.</li>
        <li>In an embedded system there is no hand to swap the two wires, so an H-bridge does it with electronic switches.</li>
        <li>Four switches surround the motor, which forms the crossbar of the H. The two upper ones connect to the motor supply VM, the two lower ones to ground.</li>
    </ul>
    <caption>Whenever you work with a motor there is going to be an H-bridge involved.</caption>
    <note>The reading's sketch. Supply at the top, ground at the bottom, motor across the middle — that is the whole shape.</note>
</slide>

            <figure xml:id="fig-hbridge-pmos-nmos">
                <caption>The four switches are power MOSFETs, and they are not all the same
                kind.  The upper two are P-channel devices, which conduct when their gate is
                driven LOW; the lower two are N-channel devices, which conduct when their gate
                is driven HIGH.  The gate of each is marked <em>g</em>.  That opposition is
                what the next page turns into a single control line per side.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day11-Motors/hbridge-pmos-nmos.svg" width="90%"/>
            </figure>
            <slide xml:id="sl-day11-pmos-nmos" ref="fig-hbridge-pmos-nmos">
    <caption>Two kinds of switch, opposite to each other: the upper pMOS conducts on a LOW gate, the lower nMOS on a HIGH gate. The gate of each is marked g.</caption>
    <note>Never mind what p and n stand for or how they work — what matters is that they are opposites, which is what the next slide rests on.</note>
</slide>

            <p>
                Closing one switch on the upper rail and the opposite switch on the lower
                rail connects one motor terminal to the supply and the other to ground, so
                current runs through the motor.  Closing the other diagonal pair connects
                the terminals the other way round, and the current — and the rotation —
                reverses.
            </p>
            <p>
                That leaves a question the four switches do not answer on their own: what
                drives their gates?  The bridge is not built with four independent controls.
                Each side is driven from a single control line — one we will call IN1 for the
                left pair and IN2 for the right pair — and the trick is that the two
                transistors on a side respond to that one line in opposite ways.  A P-channel
                switch on top turns on when its gate goes LOW, and an N-channel switch
                beneath it turns on when its gate goes HIGH, so whichever way the control
                line goes, exactly one of the pair conducts and the other is off.  One wire
                per side therefore chooses whether that terminal is tied up to the supply or
                down to ground, and two wires cover every state the motor can be put in.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-hbridge-in1-in2">
                <caption>How two control lines drive four switches.  On each side of the
                bridge the upper and lower gates are driven from the same line — IN1 on the
                left, IN2 on the right — and because the upper switch turns on when its gate
                is LOW while the lower turns on when its gate is HIGH, one control line puts
                exactly one of its pair into conduction.  Each terminal is therefore pulled
                either up to the supply or down to ground, never both.  This is the
                simplified picture; the TB6612 puts decoding logic between its input pins
                and these gates, which is what lets it also turn all four switches off.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day11-Motors/hbridge-in1-in2.svg" width="85%"/>
            </figure>
            <slide xml:id="sl-day11-in1-in2" ref="fig-hbridge-in1-in2">
    <caption>One control line per side. The upper switch turns on when its gate is LOW and the lower when it is HIGH, so exactly one of each pair conducts.</caption>
    <note>The point students need before the activity: two wires, four switches, and no way for a side to short itself.</note>
</slide>

            <activity xml:id="act-day11-diagonal">
                <title>Four settings, four things the motor can do</title>
                <introduction>
                    <p>The two control lines give four combinations, and each does something
                    different to the motor. Call the motor's terminals OUT1 (left) and OUT2 (right),
                    and recall that a control line (IN1 or IN2) HIGH pulls its terminal down to ground
                    and LOW pulls it up to the supply.</p>
                </introduction>
                <task xml:id="task-day11-diagonal-turn">
                    <statement><p>Two of the four combinations of (IN1, IN2) send current
                    through the motor.  Which two, and which switches conduct in each?  For
                    one of them, trace the path from the supply, through the motor, to ground.
                    In which way does the current cross the motor?</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day11-diagonal-short">
                    <statement><p>The other two combinations set both terminals to the
                    <em>same</em> place — both down to ground, or both up to the supply.  In
                    each case, how much voltage is across the motor, and so how much current
                    does the supply push through it?</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day11-diagonal-brake">
                    <statement><p>Take the case where both terminals are tied to ground, and
                    suppose the shaft is still spinning when you do it.  The motor is then a
                    generator with its two terminals wired together.  Using the back-EMF
                    from the motor equations, what current flows, and what does it do to the
                    rotation?  Does it matter whether you tie both terminals to ground or both
                    to the supply?</p></statement>
                </task>
            </activity>
            <instructor xml:id="inst-day11-diagonal">
                <p>The two turning combinations are the ones where the control lines differ.
                With IN1 LOW and IN2 HIGH the upper-left and lower-right switches conduct, so
                current runs supply → OUT1 → motor → OUT2 → ground, crossing the motor from
                OUT1 to OUT2.  With IN1 HIGH and IN2 LOW the other diagonal conducts and the
                current crosses the other way.  Those are the two directions of rotation.</p>
                <p>When the control lines match, both terminals go to the same rail, so the
                voltage across the motor is zero and the supply pushes no current through it.
                The motor is not being driven — but it is not disconnected either, and that
                is the interesting part.  Both of its terminals are now wired together
                through two conducting switches.  A spinning shaft still generates a
                back-EMF, and with the terminals shorted that voltage drives a current
                <m>i = e/R_a</m> around the loop.  By the first of the motor relationships, that
                current produces a torque, and it acts against the rotation that is
                generating it, so the motor is braked hard.  It makes no real difference
                whether the short is to ground or to the supply: what brakes the motor is the
                two terminals being connected to each other, not which rail they sit at.  The
                TB6612 implements this by driving both outputs LOW, which its datasheet calls
                <em>short brake</em>.</p>
                <p>Worth drawing out, because the truth table will look inconsistent with
                this sketch otherwise. With the gates tied to two control lines as drawn, every
                one of the four combinations either drives the motor or shorts it.  There is no
                way for two wires to ask for all four switches to be off at once, so the
                simplified bridge cannot leave the motor genuinely disconnected.  The real chip
                can, and that is one of the things its control logic is for — it decodes the
                inputs onto four separate gate signals rather than tying them in pairs.  So the
                mode for a given pair of inputs is read off the datasheet's table, not off this
                drawing.</p>
            </instructor>
            <slide xml:id="sl-day11-diagonals" ref="fig-hbridge-cw-ccw">
    <caption>The two combinations where the control lines differ. Current crosses the motor one way, then the other.</caption>
</slide>

            <figure xml:id="fig-hbridge-cw-ccw">
                <caption>The two driving diagonals.  On the left, the upper-left and
                lower-right switches conduct, so current flows from the supply through
                OUT1, through the motor, and back through OUT2 to ground — one direction of
                rotation.  On the right, the other diagonal conducts, and the current, and
                the rotation, are reversed.</caption>
                <sidebyside widths="48% 48%" margins="1%" valign="middle">
                    <image source="images/Day11-Motors/hbridge-cur-out1-out2.svg"/>
                    <image source="images/Day11-Motors/hbridge-cur-out2-out1.svg"/>
                </sidebyside>
            </figure>
            <p>
                The braking case and the case where the motor is simply let go are worth
                separating, because they look the same on a truth table and feel completely
                different on the bench.
            </p>
            <slide xml:id="sl-day11-brake-vs-stop" ref="fig-hbridge-brake-vs-stop">
    <caption>On the left, braking: the two terminals are shorted, so the back-EMF drives a current that opposes the rotation. The loop is dashed and carries no arrow because which way that current runs depends on which way the shaft was turning. On the right, stopping: all four switches are off, the terminals are left floating, and the shaft coasts.</caption>
    <note>Worth saying out loud: a wheel that must hold position wants the brake, a flywheel that should spin down gently wants the coast.</note>
</slide>

            <figure xml:id="fig-hbridge-brake-vs-stop">
                <caption>Braking, on the left, against stopping, on the right.  To brake, the
                two lower switches conduct, so both motor terminals are tied together at
                ground and the spinning motor's own back-EMF drives a current through that
                short, which opposes the rotation and brings the shaft quickly to rest.  That
                loop is drawn dashed and without an arrow on purpose: which way the current
                runs depends on which way the shaft happened to be turning, and either way it
                is driven to ground through the lower switches and opposes the rotation.  To
                stop, all four switches are off, so both terminals are left floating: no
                current can flow in or out, nothing opposes the rotation, and the shaft coasts
                down under friction alone.  This all-off state is one the driver's control
                logic produces; it is not something the two direction lines could ask for by
                themselves, which is why the modes are read from the datasheet's table rather
                than from the bridge drawing.</caption>
                <sidebyside widths="48% 48%" margins="1%" valign="middle">
                    <image source="images/Day11-Motors/hbridge-brake.svg"/>
                    <image source="images/Day11-Motors/hbridge-stop.svg"/>
                </sidebyside>
            </figure>
            <p>
                So there are two different ways to stop a motor, and which one we choose is a
                design decision.  Braking dissipates the shaft's kinetic energy in the
                motor's own windings and stops it in a fraction of the time.  Stopping — the
                datasheet calls it <em>stop</em>, and it is what the bridge does when all four
                switches are off — leaves the shaft free and lets it coast.  A wheel that
                must hold position wants the brake; a flywheel that should spin down gently
                wants the coast.
            </p>
            <insight>
                <title>Shoot-through</title>
                <p>
                    One combination is dangerous: the upper and lower switch on the
                    <em>same</em> side conducting at once, which the two control lines cannot
                    ask for.  That connects the supply straight to ground through
                    the two switches, with only their own resistance to limit the current — a
                    near short called <term>shoot-through</term>, which can destroy them.
                    The control lines make it impossible to command, because one line drives
                    both gates of a side in opposite senses.  It happens in the
                    <em>transitions</em> instead: a real transistor takes time to turn off,
                    so if one begins conducting before its partner has finished turning off,
                    both are briefly on together.  Reversing the motor is exactly when that
                    is most likely, because both sides switch at once.
                </p>
                <p>
                    The TB6612 contains logic that staggers its switching so every transition
                    passes through a brief state with one side fully off — one reason we drive
                    a motor through a driver IC instead of wiring the H-bridge by hand.
                </p>
            </insight>
            <slide xml:id="sl-day11-shoot-through" ref="fig-hbridge-shoot-through">
    <ul>
        <li>If we allowed the upper and lower switch on the same side to conduct at once this would short the motor supply straight to ground, with only the transistors' own resistance to limit the current.</li>
        <li>By design this is not a setting that can be obtained via the two control lines, because one line drives both gates of a side in opposite senses.</li>
        <li>But it could happens in the transitions instead: a transistor takes time to turn off, so one can still be conducting as its partner turns on. Reversing the motor is when both sides switch at once, so it is the most likely moment.</li>
        <li>The TB6612 staggers its switching so every transition passes through a brief state with one side fully off.</li>
    </ul>
    <caption>This is one reason we drive a motor through a driver IC instead of wiring the bridge by hand.</caption>
</slide>

            <figure xml:id="fig-hbridge-shoot-through">
                <caption>Shoot-through.  The upper and lower switch on the right-hand side
                are conducting at the same time, so current runs straight from the motor
                supply VM to ground without passing through the motor at all.  Nothing in
                that path limits the current except the resistance of the two transistors,
                which is why the state destroys them.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day11-Motors/hbridge-shoot-through.svg" width="75%"/>
            </figure>
        </subsection>

        <!-- Part 3 -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day11-tb6612">
            <title>Part 3: The TB6612 Driver and Its Truth Table</title>
            <p>
                The <term>TB6612</term> packages two complete H-bridges — the switches
                themselves and the logic that prevents shoot-through — onto one small
                <term>breakout board</term>, a circuit board with the chip already soldered
                down and its pins brought out to header pins we can wire by hand.  It
                exposes each bridge through a few logic-level pins, driven at the ordinary
                0 V and 3.3 V logic levels which we will drive via the STM32C031C6.
            </p>
            <p>
                The motor's own power does not come through those pins, and this is the whole
                reason the driver exists.  The chip has a separate set of terminals for it: an
                input pin VM, which we connect to an external supply, and the two output pins
                that go to the motor.  The motor equations give the reason we cannot skip this.  A stopped
                motor draws <m>V/R_a</m> at the instant it is switched on, which for our motor
                is hundreds of milliamps, where a GPIO pin can source a few — so the current
                the motor needs is fetched from the external supply through the driver's
                switches, and the microcontroller only ever tells those switches what to do.
                Each bridge can pass roughly 1.2 A to its motor; draw much more than that and
                the chip protects itself by shutting down until it cools.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-tb6612-internal">
                <caption>Inside the TB6612 — the full chip, which holds two independent
                motor channels, A and B.  We will only use channel A, but if you wanted to control several
                motors independently you could use both channels for this purpose. Channel A's three logic
                inputs are labeled AIN1, AIN2, and PWMA on the breakout (also referred to as IN1, IN2, and PWM
                below). These pass through the control logic that drives the four switch
                gates, and the switches connect channel A's two outputs (AO1, AO2) to the
                motor supply VM or to ground.  The motor's large current flows only in the
                output half; the input side is ordinary 3.3 V logic.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day11-Motors/tb6612-block-annotated.png" width="100%"/>
            </figure>
            <slide xml:id="sl-day11-tb6612-inside" ref="fig-tb6612-internal">
    <caption>Logic inputs at 3.3 V on one side — AIN1, AIN2 and PWMA on the breakout, which the datasheet's table calls IN1, IN2 and PWM. On the other side the motor's own supply enters at VM, and each bridge can pass roughly 1.2 A. A stopped motor draws V/R_a at the instant it is switched on, which for our motor is hundreds of milliamps where a GPIO pin can source a few.</caption>
    <note>The separation is the point of the driver, and it is why the motor's power does not come through the microcontroller's pins.</note>
</slide>

            <p>
                The two inputs, IN1 and IN2, choose what the bridge does, which yields the
                motor direction, and a third, PWM, controls the average voltage at the motor
                terminals by rapidly switching it on and off for speed control, which we come to next.  A
                fourth pin, STBY (standby), must be HIGH for the driver to do anything at
                all; on our breakout it is pulled up already.  How the inputs map to the four
                modes — clockwise, counter-clockwise, brake, and stop — can be found in the
                <url href="external/datasheets/TB6612FNG_datasheet_en_20141001.pdf">TB6612FNG
                datasheet</url>, in the table named <q>H-SW Control Function</q> on page 4.
            </p>
            <slide xml:id="sl-day11-four-inputs">
    <ul>
        <li>Two of the driver's logic inputs, <term>IN1</term> and <term>IN2</term>, choose what the bridge does — which is what sets the motor's direction.</li>
        <li>A third, <term>PWM</term>, sets the average voltage at the motor terminals by switching it on and off quickly. We'll discuss this shortly.</li>
        <li>A fourth, <term>STBY</term> (standby), must be HIGH for the driver to do anything at all. On our breakout it is pulled up already, so we never wire it.</li>
        <li>How those inputs map to the four modes is a table in the datasheet, on page 4.</li>
    </ul>
    <note>STBY is the one they have not met. It has a column in the table they are about to read, and the last row is the standby row.</note>
</slide>

            <figure xml:id="fig-tb6612-truth-table">
                <caption>The TB6612 <q>H-SW Control Function</q> table, from page 4 of the
                <url href="external/datasheets/TB6612FNG_datasheet_en_20141001.pdf">TB6612FNG
                datasheet</url>.  IN1, IN2, and PWM set the outputs OUT1 and OUT2, and so the
                mode.  <q>H/L</q> in a cell means the value in that position does not matter
                for that row.  </caption>
                <image source="images/Day11-Motors/tb6612-truth-table-annotated.png" width="85%"/>
            </figure>
            <slide xml:id="sl-day11-truth-table" ref="fig-tb6612-truth-table">
    <caption>Read the mode off the table, not off the bridge drawing. <q>H/L</q> in a cell means the value in that position does not matter for that row.</caption>
</slide>

            <activity xml:id="act-day11-truth-table">
                <title>What the table says that the mode names do not</title>
                <introduction>
                    <p>Work from the <q>H-SW Control Function</q> table
                    (<xref ref="fig-tb6612-truth-table"/>), with STBY HIGH throughout, and
                    compare it against what you worked out about the bridge.</p>
                </introduction>
                <task xml:id="task-day11-tt-pwm-low">
                    <statement><p>Select a direction — say IN1 HIGH and IN2 LOW — and then
                    read the row that with PWM HIGH.
                    What are OUT1 and OUT2 and what mode does the table name it? How does that compare to your earlier prediction?</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day11-tt-brake-stop">
                    <statement><p>The table shows both a <em>short brake</em> mode and a
                    <em>stop</em> mode.  Find the input combination for each, and read off
                    OUT1 and OUT2.  One of them describes the outputs as
                    <q>OFF (High impedance)</q> — explain what that phrase means about the
                    four switches, and match the two rows to the two pictures of braking and stopping.</p></statement>
                </task>
            </activity>
            <instructor xml:id="inst-day11-truth-table">
                <p>With IN1 HIGH and IN2 LOW: PWM HIGH gives OUT1 HIGH, OUT2 LOW, which the
                table names CW.  PWM LOW gives OUT1 LOW and OUT2 LOW, which the table names
                <em>short brake</em> — the same shorted-terminals state we reasoned out from the bridge, not a disconnection.  So when we drive PWM with a square wave, the
                motor is not alternating between driven and free; it alternates between driven
                and braked.  That is a real effect and a useful one: the braked phase holds the
                current in the windings rather than letting it collapse, which is part of why a
                fast PWM waveform produces smooth torque rather than a series of kicks.</p>
                <p>Short brake is IN1 HIGH, IN2 HIGH, with both outputs driven LOW — both
                terminals tied to ground through the lower switches, the left-hand picture in
                <xref ref="fig-hbridge-brake-vs-stop"/>.  Stop is IN1 LOW, IN2 LOW, and its
                outputs are <q>OFF (High impedance)</q>, which means all four switches are off
                and neither terminal is connected to anything — the right-hand picture.  This
                is the row where the simplified bridge would have misled them: two
                gates tied to a LOW control line would both pull their terminals up to VM, a
                short, whereas the real chip's logic turns everything off instead.  High
                impedance is the phrase datasheets use for a pin that is neither driven HIGH
                nor driven LOW but simply disconnected, so it can neither source nor sink
                current.</p>
                <p>Driving STBY LOW puts the whole chip into standby: the last row's H/L
                entries say that whatever IN1, IN2 and PWM are doing, the outputs are off and
                the motor is unpowered.  A MOSFET gate that is left floating sits at whatever
                voltage stray charge and leakage give it, so an unconnected STBY pin would
                leave the driver in an undefined state — enabled sometimes, disabled others.
                The breakout's pull-up resistor removes the question by holding it HIGH unless
                something deliberately pulls it down.</p>
            </instructor>
        </subsection>

        <!-- Part 4 -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day11-direction">
            <title>Part 4: First Exercise — Direction by Hand</title>
            <p>
                Now we wire the driver and a motor and drive it directly, with no program
                yet, so that the modes in the truth table become things you have seen the
                motor do.  The motor needs its own current path and more than the 3.3 V logic
                can give, so it runs from a separate supply.  A 6–12 V wall adapter plugs
                into the barrel connector of a small regulator board; on that board a
                regulator brings the input down to a steady 5 V and brings it out on a
                header, and we feed that 5 V to the driver's VM pin.  The driver's logic
                side, VCC, runs from the Nucleo's 3.3 V.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-tb6612-regulator">
                <caption>The regulator board that supplies the motor, seen close up.  The
                barrel connector at the bottom takes the 6–12 V wall adapter; the regulator
                is the black component in the middle, with a capacitor either side of it; and
                each of the two headers at the top brings out three pins, labeled 5V, GND and
                Vin.  Take the motor supply from the <em>5V</em> pin.  The <em>Vin</em> pin is
                the raw, unregulated input from the adapter, and wiring it to VM would drive
                the motor at up to 12 V rather than its rated 3–6 V.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day11-Motors/slide17_img2.png" width="26%"/>
            </figure>
            <slide xml:id="sl-day11-regulator" ref="fig-tb6612-regulator">
    <ul>
        <li>The motor needs its own current path, and more than the 3.3 V logic rail can give. So it runs from a separate supply, and not from the Nucleo. We use the voltage regulator shown on the right.</li>
        <li>A 6–12 V wall adapter plugs into the barrel connector; the regulator brings that down to a steady 5 V and brings it out on a header (pin labeled 5V at the top).</li>
        <li>That 5 V is what we feed to the motor driver's <term>VM</term> pin — the motor supply, the rail across the top of every H-bridge drawing today. The driver's logic side, VCC, on the other hand runs from the Nucleo's 3.3 V.</li>
        <li>Note: do not use the <term>Vin</term> pin which is the raw, unregulated input. Wiring that to VM would drive the motor at up to 12 V rather than its rated 3–6 V.</li>
    </ul>
    <caption>Two supplies, one ground: 5 V for the motor, 3.3 V for the logic.</caption>
</slide>

            <p>
                The 5 V motor supply and the 3.3 V logic supply share a common ground — one
                wire joins the regulator board's GND to the Nucleo's ground — but are
                otherwise separate.  That separation is crucial to get right: the motor is supplied from the 5V output of the voltage regulator, the logic pins from the Nucleo. All 5 V wires need to stay on the motor side and never touch the 3.3 V rail. That way a
                mistake on the motor side stays on the motor side and does not reach the
                3.3 V parts of the board.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-tb6612-wiring">
                <caption>The circuit for the first exercise.  The TB6612 breakout takes the
                regulated 5 V at VM, the Nucleo's 3.3 V at VCC, and a ground shared with the
                Nucleo; the motor connects to the two channel-A outputs.  The 0.22 µF
                capacitor across the breakout's logic supply is a smoothing capacitor: it sits
                between VCC and ground and holds that rail steady when the motor's switching
                disturbs it.  For this exercise the PWM pin is held HIGH and the two direction
                wires, AIN1 and AIN2, are left unconnected, so that you can move them by hand
                between the power and ground rails.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day11-Motors/tb6612-wiring-exercise1.png" width="100%"/>
            </figure>
            <slide xml:id="sl-day11-wiring-1" ref="fig-tb6612-wiring">
    <caption>Nothing powered while you wire it — no USB, no adapter — and have your wiring checked before you apply power. The two supplies share a ground and are otherwise separate: every 5 V wire stays on the motor side and never touches the 3.3 V rail. After the check, USB first for the 3.3 V logic, then the wall adapter for the motor's 5 V.</caption>
    <note>The figure shows the while-wiring state, which is why it says to leave USB disconnected; the order after the check is USB first, then the adapter.</note>
</slide>

            <p>
                The truth table again, to work from while you wire and test.
            </p>
            <image source="images/Day11-Motors/tb6612-truth-table-annotated.png" width="70%"/>

            <activity xml:id="act-day11-direction">
                <title>Drive the motor's direction by hand</title>
                <introduction>
                    <p>Build the circuit with the power off, have your wiring checked by an instructor before
                    you apply power, and then work through the settings by hand.  Keep the
                    <q>H-SW Control Function</q> table in front of you.</p>
                </introduction>
                <task xml:id="task-day11-dir-wire">
                    <statement><p>With everything unpowered, wire it up as in the image above, paying special attention to the regulator's 5 V only going to the motor driver's VM pin whereas the motor driver's VCC
                    is supplied by the Nucleo's 3.3 V. The regulator and the Nucleo share a common ground.  Connect the motor to the two channel-A
                    outputs of the motor driver.  Tie the PWM pin HIGH, to the 3.3 V rail, and leave the AIN1 and
                    AIN2 wires dangling free for now.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day11-dir-predict">
                    <statement><p>You will move AIN1 and AIN2 between the 3.3 V rail (call it
                    <q>+</q>) and ground (<q>&#8722;</q>).  Using the truth table, predict for
                    each of the four settings of (AIN1, AIN2) — (+, +), (+, &#8722;),
                    (&#8722;, +) and (&#8722;, &#8722;) — both what the motor will do and what
                    voltages you would measure at the two motor terminals.  Write all four
                    predictions down before applying power.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day11-dir-verify">
                    <statement><p>Once your wiring has been checked, power the circuit — plug
                    the wall adapter into the regulator board for the motor's 5 V, and connect
                    the Nucleo to your computer over USB for the 3.3 V logic — then move the
                    AIN1 and AIN2 wires through all four settings.  Does the motor do what you
                    predicted each time?  If the motor does nothing in any of the four
                    settings, check that the regulator's 5 V actually reaches VM before
                    checking the individual wires; if a single setting is wrong, check that one
                    wire against the truth table.</p></statement>
                </task>
            </activity>
            <instructor xml:id="inst-day11-direction">
                <p>With PWM held HIGH: (+, +), both inputs HIGH, is short brake, with both
                outputs LOW; (+, &#8722;) is IN1 HIGH, IN2 LOW, one direction of rotation,
                with OUT1 HIGH and OUT2 LOW; (&#8722;, +) is the other direction, OUT1 LOW and
                OUT2 HIGH; and (&#8722;, &#8722;), both LOW, is stop, with both outputs off
                and high-impedance so the motor coasts.  The two settings where the inputs
                differ are the two turning diagonals; the two where they match are
                the brake and the coast.</p>
                <p>One thing to expect and to mention: which physical direction counts as
                clockwise depends on which way round the two motor wires are connected, so
                half the room will see the datasheet's CW as counter-clockwise.  Nothing is
                wrong; swapping the two motor wires swaps the labels.</p>
            </instructor>

            <activity xml:id="act-day11-direction-more">
                <title>If you finish early: measure what the table promised</title>
                <introduction>
                    <p>These two measurements are worth doing once the motor turns on command.</p>
                </introduction>
                <task xml:id="task-day11-dir-measure">
                    <statement><p>Confirm the other half of your prediction.  Put the
                    oscilloscope on each of the two motor terminals in turn and measure them
                    for the two turning settings, for (+, +) and for (&#8722;, &#8722;).  Do
                    the measurements match the OUT1 and OUT2 columns of the truth table?  One
                    of the four settings behaves differently from the others when you measure
                    it — which, and can you say from your measurement what
                    <q>OFF (High impedance)</q> looks like on a scope?</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day11-dir-brake">
                    <statement><p>Two of the settings stop a spinning motor, and we reasoned that they stop it in very different ways.  Get the motor up to speed in one
                    direction, then switch to (+, +) and watch how long the shaft takes to
                    come to rest; get it up to speed again and switch to (&#8722;, &#8722;)
                    instead.  Which stops it faster, and does that match what you predicted
                    about braking against coasting?</p></statement>
                </task>
            </activity>
            <instructor xml:id="inst-day11-direction-more">
                <p>The measurements should give roughly 5 V and 0 V at the two terminals for
                the turning settings, and 0 V at both for (+, +).  The odd one out is
                (&#8722;, &#8722;): with all four switches off, neither terminal is driven at
                all, so the scope shows a floating node rather than a clean voltage — it may
                drift, sit at some in-between level, or follow the still-spinning motor's own
                back-EMF as it coasts down.  That is what high impedance looks like: the pin
                is not holding any voltage, so whatever is connected to it decides.</p>
                <p>Braking, at (+, +), stops the shaft in a small fraction of the time that
                coasting at (&#8722;, &#8722;) takes, and the heavier the flywheel on the shaft
                the more obvious the difference.  It is the clearest demonstration of the day
                that back-EMF is real: the only thing stopping the motor quickly is the
                current its own rotation drives through the shorted windings.</p>
            </instructor>
            <p>
                One caution about the direction names.  The truth table calls its two turning
                modes CW and CCW, but the chip has no way of knowing which way round you
                connected the motor's two wires, so the direction you actually see may be the
                opposite of the one the table names.  That is not a fault, and if it bothers
                you, swapping the motor's two wires swaps which mode turns it which way.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <!-- Part 5 -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day11-pwm">
            <title>Part 5: PWM Sets the Speed</title>
            <p>
                The H-bridge sets the motor's direction.  In this part we turn to its
                speed, which we set with <term>pulse-width modulation</term> (PWM).  We have
                done this once already: the <c>SevenSeg_dim()</c> function we wrote on Day 10
                pulsed the display's LEDs, and a wider pulse gave a brighter digit.  The same
                idea sets a motor's speed.  The
                reading showed the difficulty: a GPIO pin
                is either fully HIGH or fully LOW, with nothing in between, so it cannot hold
                an in-between voltage.  Nor does the STM32C031C6 have a true DAC to do it for
                us — the DAC we met on Day 7 lived inside the ADC, making test voltages the
                program never sees, not a pin we can drive to an analog level.  So rather
                than hold the motor at some steady in-between voltage, we switch the full
                supply on and off quickly and let the <term>duty cycle</term> — the fraction
                of each period spent HIGH — set the average.  The motor's inertia smooths the
                pulses, and it turns as though driven by that average voltage.
            </p>
            <slide xml:id="sl-day11-pwm-idea">
    <p>The H-bridge sets the motor's direction. In this part we turn to its speed, which we set with pulse-width modulation (PWM).</p>
    <ul>
        <li>We have done this once already: dimming the seven-segment display on Day 10 pulsed its LEDs, and a wider pulse gave a higher duty cycle and a brighter light. The same idea sets a motor's speed.</li>
        <li>A GPIO pin is either fully HIGH or fully LOW, so it cannot hold an in-between voltage, and the STM32C031C6 has no true DAC to create one for us.</li>
        <li>So we switch the full supply on and off quickly, and let the fraction of each period spent HIGH — the <term>duty cycle</term> — set the average voltage.</li>
        <li>The motor's inertia smooths the pulses, and the shaft turns as though it were driven by that average.</li>
    </ul>
</slide>

            <figure xml:id="fig-pwm-waveform">
                <caption>PWM at three duty cycles, drawn for a 5 V supply.  The output is a
                fixed-frequency square wave; the wider the HIGH pulse, the higher the
                average voltage (dashed line) and the faster the motor.  Here a 75% duty
                cycle averages 3.75 V, a 50% duty cycle averages 2.5 V, and a 25% duty
                cycle averages 1.25 V.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day11-Motors/pwm-duty-cycles.svg" width="70%"/>
            </figure>
            <slide xml:id="sl-day11-duty-cycles" ref="fig-pwm-waveform">
    <caption>Same period in all three; only the width of the HIGH pulse changes. The average voltage is proportional to the duty cycle: V_PWM = V_SUPPLY × T_HIGH / T.</caption>
</slide>

            <p>
                The waveform is produced with almost no work.  We use a counter that counts up from
                zero and resets when it reaches a fixed top value, over and over.  A second
                number, the compare value, is checked against the counter: while the counter
                is below the compare value the output is HIGH, and once it reaches the
                compare value the output goes LOW until the counter resets.  A larger compare
                value keeps the output HIGH for more of each period — a larger duty cycle.
                The entire process runs in the timer hardware, so once it is set up the CPU does
                nothing to keep it going. The output is directed to one of the microcontroller pins from where it can direct the motor speed.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-pwm-counter-compare">
                <caption>How the timer makes the waveform.  A counter counts up and resets
                at its top value, over and over (the blue ramp).  A compare value (the
                dashed line) is checked against it: while the counter is below the compare
                value the output is HIGH, and above it the output is LOW.  Raising the
                compare value keeps the ramp below the line for more of each period, which
                widens the HIGH pulse and so raises the duty cycle.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day11-Motors/pwm-counter-compare.svg" width="80%"/>
            </figure>
            <slide xml:id="sl-day11-counter-compare" ref="fig-pwm-counter-compare" room="compressed">
    <ul>
        <li>A counter counts up from zero and resets when it reaches a fixed top value, over and over. That top value is the timer's ARR, the same auto-reload register we set on Day 8.</li>
        <li>A second number, the compare value, is checked against the counter. While the counter is below it the output is HIGH, and once the counter reaches it the output goes LOW until the counter resets. That number lives in the timer's CCR1, the capture/compare register.</li>
        <li>A larger compare value keeps the output HIGH for more of each period, which is a larger duty cycle.</li>
        <li>The entire process runs in the timer hardware, so once it is set up the CPU does nothing to keep it going. The output goes to a microcontroller pin, and from there it sets the motor's speed.</li>
    </ul>
    <note>All of it runs in the timer hardware, so once it is set up the CPU does nothing to keep it going.</note>
</slide>

            <p>
                We'll use timer TIM14 for this — the same timer we set up for periodic
                interrupts on Day 8 — and route its output onto a pin.  Not every pin can
                carry a given timer's output; the datasheet's pin table lists which can.
            </p>
            <slide xml:id="sl-day11-tim14-pin">
    <ul>
        <li>We'll use timer TIM14, the same timer we used for periodic interrupts on Day 8.</li>
        <li>Almost every pin can carry a PWM output, driven by one counter or another; the datasheet's Table 12 shows which counter to use for a particular pin, and which alternate function mode.</li>
    </ul>
    <note>This is the table they go to in the first task of the activity, so leave it on the wall while they start.</note>
</slide>

            <activity xml:id="act-day11-pwm">
                <title>Getting ready to program the timer</title>
                <introduction>
                    <p>We will drive the PWM from pin PA7 (Arduino header D11). </p>
                </introduction>
                <task xml:id="task-day11-pwm-pin">
                    <statement><p>In the STM32C031C6 datasheet — not the TB6612 one — find
                    Table 12, <em>Pin assignment and description</em>, on page 30.  Look up PA7
                    and read off which timer channel it can output, and which of the pin's
                    alternate functions it is.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day11-pwm-config">
                    <statement><p>A pin only carries a timer's output once it has been switched
                    away from being an ordinary GPIO output and handed to that peripheral,
                    which is the same alternate-function machinery we used to give pins to
                    the UART and the ADC.  Write the register operations that put PA7 into
                    alternate-function mode and select the alternate function you found above.
                    Name the registers and the bit positions; you do not need the timer's own
                    registers for this.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day11-pwm-timing">
                    <statement><p>Now the timer's numbers.  Recall that our system clock runs at 12 MHz.
                    We want to create a PWM signal at a switching frequency of about 1.6 kHz. Furthermore,
                    each period should be divided into 1250 steps of duty cycle.  What must the prescaler divide the
                    clock by, and what value does the counter have to count to?  Remember from
                    our earlier work with timers what actually gets written into the prescaler
                    and auto-reload registers, as opposed to the number you have in
                    mind.</p></statement>
                </task>
            </activity>
            <instructor xml:id="inst-day11-pwm">
                <p>Table 12 gives PA7 the alternate function TIM14_CH1, which is alternate
                function 4.</p>
                <p>Two registers, in the clear-then-set idiom the course uses throughout.  The
                mode register selects alternate-function mode for pin 7, and the low
                alternate-function register selects AF4 for pin 7:</p>
                <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
RCC->IOPENR |= RCC_IOPENR_GPIOAEN;          // Enable clock access to GPIOA

GPIOA->MODER &= ~GPIO_MODER_MODE7_Msk;      // PA7 alternate function mode (10)
GPIOA->MODER |= GPIO_ALTERNATE << GPIO_MODER_MODE7_Pos;

GPIOA->AFR[0] &= ~GPIO_AFRL_AFSEL7_Msk;     // PA7 alt. function type (AF4, 0100)
GPIOA->AFR[0] |= GPIO_AF4 << GPIO_AFRL_AFSEL7_Pos;
]]></code></program>
                <p>The AFR array is worth a word: there are two alternate-function registers,
                a low one for pins 0 to 7 and a high one for pins 8 to 15, so PA7 is the last
                pin handled by <c>AFR[0]</c>.</p>
                <p>The timing.  We need 1250 counts per PWM period at 1.6 kHz, so the counter
                must be clocked at 1250 × 1600 Hz = 2 MHz, and the prescaler must therefore
                divide the 12 MHz system clock by 6.  What goes into the registers is each of
                those numbers minus one, because the prescaler and the counter both start from
                zero: the prescaler register gets 5, and the auto-reload register gets 1249.
                That is exactly what the demonstration program does, and next class they
                will write it.</p>
            </instructor>
        </subsection>

        <!-- Part 6 -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day11-demo">
            <title>Part 6: Second Exercise — Ramping the Motor with PWM</title>
            <p>
                For the last exercise we hand the driver's inputs over to the Nucleo and run
                a program that ramps the motor's speed up and down and reverses it.  The
                wiring continues from the first exercise: leave the power, the motor, and the
                breakout as they are, and move three wires.  The PWM pin, which you tied HIGH
                by hand, now goes to PA7 (D11); the AIN1 and AIN2 wires, which you moved by
                hand, now go to PA5 (D13) and PA6 (D12), so the program controls all three.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-tb6612-wiring-2">
                <caption>Wiring for the second exercise, changed from the first only at the
                driver's three logic inputs: PWM to PA7 (D11), AIN1 to PA5 (D13), AIN2 to
                PA6 (D12).  The motor and its 5 V supply are unchanged.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day11-Motors/tb6612-wiring-exercise2.png" width="80%"/>
            </figure>
            <slide xml:id="sl-day11-wiring-2" ref="fig-tb6612-wiring-2">
    <ul>
        <li>Take the PWM wire, the one that was connected to logic power, and connect it to PA7.</li>
        <li>Then take the two dangling wires and connect them to PA5 and PA6.</li>
        <li>We'll use the PWM on PA7 to change the speed of the motor, and PA5 and PA6 to determine its direction.</li>
    </ul>
    <caption>The motor and its 5 V supply stay exactly as they are; only these three wires move.</caption>
</slide>

            <p>
                Make a copy of <c>TemplateProject</c>, rename it <c>DCmotor</c>, and add the
                demonstration program <c>TTmotor_ramp.c</c> to its <c>Src</c> folder.
                Compile and run it.  The program sets up TIM14 for PWM on PA7, drives AIN1
                and AIN2 for direction, and repeatedly ramps the duty cycle up and back down,
                reversing the motor at the ends of the ramp.  You will run it today; we will
                go through how it programs TIM14 in the next class.
            </p>
            <slide xml:id="sl-day11-run-demo">
    <p>Copy your <c>TemplateProject</c>, rename it <c>DCmotor</c>, and add <c>TTmotor_ramp.c</c> to its <c>Src</c> folder, then compile and run it.</p>
    <ul>
        <li>The program sets up TIM14 for PWM on PA7, drives AIN1 and AIN2 for direction, and ramps the duty cycle up and back down, reversing the motor at the ends of the ramp.</li>
        <li>You will run it today; we'll go through how it programs TIM14 tomorrow.</li>
    </ul>

</slide>

            <activity xml:id="act-day11-demo">
                <title>Measure the PWM, and account for what the motor does</title>
                <introduction>
                    <p>With the program running and the motor turning, put the oscilloscope
                    on the PWM signal: channel 1 (the orange lead) on PA7, and its ground lead
                    to a ground on the board.  If the motor does not move when the program
                    runs, confirm the build succeeded and that the three wires reached PA5,
                    PA6, and PA7.</p>
                </introduction>
                <task xml:id="task-day11-demo-period">
                    <statement><p>Measure the period of the waveform, and from it the PWM
                    frequency.  The timer's counter is fed a 2 MHz clock — the 12 MHz system
                    clock divided by 6 — and counts to 1250 before resetting.  Work out the
                    frequency those two numbers predict, and compare it with what you
                    measured.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day11-demo-duty">
                    <statement><p>Freeze the trace at a moment when the motor is running at a
                    middling speed and measure the width of the HIGH pulse as well as the
                    period.  Give the duty cycle as a percentage, and then convert it into the
                    compare value out of 1250 that the program must have written to produce
                    it.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day11-demo-reversal">
                    <statement><p>Watch what happens at the end of a ramp, where the motor
                    reverses.  The program does not simply swap the direction inputs: it brakes
                    the motor first, then applies the opposite direction.  Give two independent
                    reasons why this is worth doing — one about the switches inside the driver, and
                    one about the current through the motor. What you would expect to
                    go wrong if the program reversed the direction inputs at full speed
                    instead?</p></statement>
                </task>
            </activity>
            <instructor xml:id="inst-day11-demo">
                <p>The period measures about 625 µs, and 1/625 µs = 1.6 kHz.  The prediction
                from the timer's numbers agrees: 12,000,000 Hz ÷ 6 = 2,000,000 Hz feeds the
                counter, and 2,000,000 Hz ÷ 1250 = 1,600 Hz = 1.6 kHz.</p>
                <p>For the duty-cycle measurement, a HIGH pulse of, say, 250 µs in a 625 µs
                period is 40%, and 40% of 1250 is a compare value of 500.  Any consistent pair
                of numbers is fine; what matters is that they convert the measured width into
                the compare value the program wrote, which is the number they will compute
                and load on Day 11x.</p>
                <p>The reversal.  The first reason is shoot-through: reversing means both sides
                of the bridge switch at once, which is exactly when a transistor that has not
                finished turning off can conduct at the same time as its partner.  Braking
                first puts the bridge into a known state with both outputs LOW, so each side
                switches once, separately, instead of both switching together.  The second
                reason is the current through the motor.  A motor spinning at speed has a
                back-EMF of nearly the supply voltage; reverse the terminals while it is still
                spinning and the supply and the back-EMF now add rather than oppose, so the
                voltage across the armature resistance is close to twice the supply and the
                current is roughly twice the stall current — a large current spike
                through both the driver and the motor, in a chip rated for about 1.2 A.
                Braking first lets the shaft slow down, which lowers the back-EMF before the
                reversal, so the spike is much smaller.  Expect a student to get one of these;
                the second is the one worth drawing out.</p>
            </instructor>
            <figure xml:id="fig-pwm-scope">
                <caption>The PWM signal from <c>TTmotor_ramp.c</c> on the oscilloscope, while
                the ramp runs.  The window is narrow enough to hold only a few periods of the
                1.6 kHz waveform, so what you are watching is one pulse train changing shape:
                the width of the HIGH portion grows and shrinks as the motor speeds up and
                slows down, while the spacing from one rising edge to the next — the period —
                does not move at all.  That is duty cycle changing while frequency stays
                fixed, which is the whole of how this program sets the speed.</caption>
                <video youtube="MuDX2vrNV3Q" width="85%"/>
            </figure>
            <slide xml:id="sl-day11-scope" ref="fig-pwm-scope">
    <caption>Watch the HIGH portion grow and shrink while the period stays exactly where it is.</caption>
</slide>

            <activity xml:id="act-day11-stretch">
                <title>Going further</title>
                <introduction>
                    <p>These two questions go further than the measurements above, and are optional.</p>
                </introduction>
                <task xml:id="task-day11-stretch-resolution">
                    <statement><p>The counter resets at 1250, so the duty cycle can only take
                    1250 distinct values.  Work out the smallest change in average motor
                    voltage the program can ask for.  If we had instead let the counter reset
                    at 100, what would the PWM frequency have become, and what would the
                    smallest voltage step have been?  What are the trade-offs between the two
                    choices?</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day11-stretch-lowfreq">
                    <statement><p>In Lab 6 there is an optional experiment: running the same
                    motor with a 50 Hz PWM instead of 1.6 kHz.  Work out the period of each,
                    and then think about what the motor does between one HIGH pulse and the
                    next: recall from the truth table that during the LOW part of a cycle the
                    driver is braking the motor rather than letting it coast.  Predict what
                    changes about the motor's behavior at 50 Hz — both what gets worse and what
                    might get better — before you ever try it.</p></statement>
                </task>
            </activity>
            <instructor xml:id="inst-day11-stretch">
                <p>With 1250 steps on a 5 V supply, one step of the compare value is
                5 V ÷ 1250 = 4 mV of average voltage.  Resetting at 100 instead would make the
                frequency 2,000,000 Hz ÷ 100 = 20 kHz, and the step 5 V ÷ 100 = 50 mV.  The
                trade-off: for a fixed counter clock, a higher PWM frequency and a finer speed
                resolution pull in opposite directions, because the frequency is the clock
                divided by the number of steps.</p>
                <p>At 1.6 kHz the period is 625 µs; at 50 Hz it is 20 ms, which is no longer
                short compared with the rotor's mechanical response, so the shaft can partly
                follow individual cycles — and each cycle now spends milliseconds braking:
                expect audible and felt pulsing, and rougher rotation, especially at low duty
                cycles.  What can improve is low-speed torque — each pulse is a full-voltage
                pulse lasting long enough to break the shaft loose from static friction, so a
                motor that would sit still at 5% duty at 1.6 kHz may creep at 50 Hz.  That is
                exactly the trade the Lab 6 note is pointing at.</p>
            </instructor>
            <p>
                For next class, work through <c>TTmotor_ramp.c</c> with the reference manual
                open — RM0490, <q>General-purpose timers (TIM14)</q>, section 17.3.8
                (<em>PWM mode</em>) and section 17.4 (<em>TIM14 registers</em>) — and see how
                the program sets the timer's registers to produce the PWM you watched today.
            </p>
        </subsection>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <section xml:id="sec-motors-day11x">
        <title>Day 11x In-Class: Writing the PWM Code</title>

        <introduction>
            <p>
                On Tuesday we got a motor turning.  We wired the TB6612 breakout, set the
                motor's direction by hand from the truth table, and then ran
                <c>TTmotor_ramp.c</c>, which ramped the speed up and back down while we
                watched the duty cycle change on the oscilloscope.  Today we'll open that
                program and read it one register at a time.  For any line of its setup you'll
                be able to describe which register it writes and why.  We'll work from the reference manual, RM0490,
                from the figures, and from the code.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <!-- Part 1 -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day11x-timer-peripheral">
            <title>Part 1: The Timer That Makes the Waveform</title>
            <p>
                The hardware we built on Tuesday controls both a motor's direction of
                rotation and its speed of rotation.  The H-bridge inside the TB6612 chooses
                which way current crosses the motor, which is what sets the direction;
                pulse-width modulation of the switches in that bridge sets what fraction of
                each period the supply is connected at all, which sets the average voltage
                across the terminals and so the speed.  The wiring that does both is the
                circuit you built for the second exercise
                (<xref ref="fig-tb6612-wiring-2"/>): motor power from the regulator, logic
                power from the Nucleo's 3.3 V, and three signal wires from PA5 (D13),
                PA6 (D12) and PA7 (D11).
            </p>
            <p>
                The PWM signal itself comes out of a peripheral we have already studied.  TIM14
                is the same timer we set up on Day 8 to interrupt once a millisecond, and
                there we looked only at its timing core — the prescaler that divides the system
                clock down, the counter CNT that counts the divided pulses, and the
                auto-reload register that sets how far the counter runs before it starts
                again (<xref ref="fig-tim14-block"/>).  PWM is built into that same timer, in
                the machinery around that core that we left aside on Day 8.
            </p>
            <p>
                <xref ref="fig-tim14-block-full"/> is the whole of TIM14 as the reference
                manual, RM0490, prints it in Figure 165.  The core we already know runs
                across the middle.  What is new is the block located just below the counter —
                the capture/compare register, <c>CCR1</c> — and the path leading from it out
                to the pin marked TIMx_CH1, which for us is PA7.  <c>CCR1</c> holds the
                compare value we introduced on Tuesday: the counter is compared against it on
                the way up, so <c>CCR1</c> sets how long the output stays HIGH in each period,
                while the auto-reload register sets how long the whole period is.  Today
                we'll learn about the registers that are needed to configure the timer so that
                the result of the comparison reaches the pin.
            </p>

            <!-- P-15: her annotation names functional blocks only - prescaler,
                 counter, auto-reload, compare register.  It names none of
                 CCMR1, CCER, CR1 or EGR, which is exactly why it can open the
                 day: Part 2's discoveries are not answered inside this figure.
                 DO NOT add register names to it.  The omission is deliberate. -->
            <figure xml:id="fig-tim14-block-full">
                <caption>TIM14 in full, as RM0490 Figure 165 prints it.  On Day 8 we
                looked only at this timer's core — the prescaler, the counter, and the
                auto-reload value it counts up to (<xref ref="fig-tim14-block"/>) — and said
                the capture/compare machinery around it would come back in the motor
                chapters.  This is it: now we introduce a new timer mode that allows us to
                use the timer to produce a PWM signal on a pin.  The clock still enters at the left and the counter
                CNT still runs up to the auto-reload register, but now the capture/compare
                register below it is compared against the counter on the way up, and the
                result leaves through the output control block at the right, on the pin
                marked TIMx_CH1 — which for us is PA7.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day11x-Motors(2)/fig-tim14-block-full.png" width="95%"/>
            </figure>
            <slide xml:id="sl-day11x-tim14-block" ref="fig-tim14-block-full">
    <caption>The same timer we used on Day 8, with a register we didn't use then: the compare register, and the path from it to the pin.</caption>
    <note>≈ 4 min, and it is a locate-along, not group work — show of hands. Ask for the three blocks Day 8 and Day 11 gave them numbers for: prescaler (÷6), the counter, and the top value (1250). Then point at what is new around them — the capture/compare register and the output control block — which is exactly what the timers chapter said would come back here. The point is that the timer is a thing with parts, before Part 2 goes hunting for the registers of those parts. Do not name CCMR1 or CCER here; Part 2 has to find them.</note>
</slide>
        </subsection>

        <!-- Part 2 -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day11x-registers">
            <title>Part 2: Which Register Does Each Job</title>

            <!-- DELIVERY 2: prose. -->

            <p>
                <c>TTmotor_ramp.c</c>, which we ran on Tuesday, drives exactly one pin: PA7,
                which you found on Day 11 is where TIM14's channel 1 comes out.  Getting that pin to
                carry a PWM waveform takes twelve steps of setup, and most of them write
                registers we have written before — some on Day 8, when we first used this
                timer, and some on Day 11, when we configured the pin.  So before we
                go looking for what is new, here is what we already know how to do.
            </p>
            <table xml:id="table-day11x-recall">
                <title>Setting up PA7 as a PWM output: what we have already done, and where</title>
                <tabular halign="left">
                    <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
                        <cell>To do this</cell>
                        <cell>we write</cell>
                        <cell>and we have done it before</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell>Give the pin's port a clock</cell>
                        <cell><c>RCC_IOPENR</c></cell>
                        <cell>Day 11</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell>Hand the pin to the timer</cell>
                        <cell><c>GPIOA_MODER</c>, <c>GPIOA_AFR[0]</c></cell>
                        <cell>Day 11 — you wrote these yourself</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell>Give TIM14 a clock</cell>
                        <cell><c>RCC_APBENR2</c></cell>
                        <cell>Day 8</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell>Set the counting rate</cell>
                        <cell><c>TIM14_PSC</c></cell>
                        <cell>Day 8</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell>Set the value the counter runs up to</cell>
                        <cell><c>TIM14_ARR</c></cell>
                        <cell>Day 8</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell>Start the counter</cell>
                        <cell><c>TIM14_CR1</c></cell>
                        <cell>Day 8</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row bottom="minor">
                        <cell>Set the value the counter is compared against</cell>
                        <cell><c>TIM14_CCR1</c></cell>
                        <cell>Day 11</cell>
                    </row>
                </tabular>
            </table>
            <slide xml:id="sl-day11x-recall" ref="table-day11x-recall">
    <p><c>TTmotor_ramp.c</c> drives one pin — PA7, where TIM14's channel 1 comes out. Setting it up takes twelve steps, and most of them write registers we have written before.</p>
    <caption>We'll need a few more registers to set the timer up for PWM mode.</caption>
    <note>≈ 2 min. Project this before the activity. The point is that the room is further along than it feels — and that the fifteen minutes coming up are for the three things that are genuinely new, not for re-finding PSC and ARR.</note>
</slide>

            <p>
                That leaves three things the table does not cover, and they are what we'll look up next.  Two of them are registers we have never
                written: one puts the channel into PWM mode, and one lets the channel's
                output reach the pin at all.  The third is not a missing register so much as
                a line whose reason is not obvious — the program writes one more register
                just before it starts the counter, and the interesting part is why that
                write has to happen before rather than after starting the counter.  All three are in section 17.4
                of the reference manual, and that is where we'll find them.
            </p>
            <activity xml:id="act-day11x-registers">
                <title>Which register does each job</title>
                <introduction>
                    <p>Open the
                    <url href="external/stm32c031_rm.pdf">STM32C031 reference manual</url>
                    and navigate to <em>Section 17.4, TIM14 registers</em>, on page 482 —
                    section 17 is the one titled <q>General-purpose timers (TIM14)</q>.
                    Section 17.3.8, <em>PWM mode</em>, on page 479, is a few pages earlier
                    and you will want it for the first question.</p>
                </introduction>
                <task xml:id="task-day11x-pwm-mode">
                    <statement><p>Section 17.3.8 says PWM mode is selected by writing
                    <c>0110</c> or <c>0111</c> into a field called OC1M, and section 17.4
                    tells you which register that field lives in.  Find the register.  Then
                    read what the two values do, and work out which of them holds the pin HIGH
                    from the start of each period until the counter reaches the compare
                    value, and then drops it.  If we had picked the other one, what would
                    the trace on the oscilloscope have looked like?</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day11x-cc1e">
                    <statement><p>Find the bit named CC1E, and the register it lives in.
                    How does the manual describe what it does?  Our program sets it in one line.  If
                    you deleted that one line and changed nothing else — the pin still in
                    alternate-function mode, the counter still running, the compare value
                    still being written every two seconds — what would you see on
                    PA7?</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day11x-egr">
                    <statement><p>The program writes one more register that
                    <xref ref="table-day11x-recall"/> does not list and neither of the
                    questions above asks about.  Find it in section 17.4.  It is written
                    near the end of the setup, just before the counter is started.  What is
                    it, what does the bit our program sets do, and why would you want to do
                    that before starting the counter rather than after?</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day11x-early">
                    <statement><p>If your group has answered all three of the above questions, work this out: our program
                    calls <c>tim14_pwm_set(625)</c>.  Given that the counter runs up to
                    1249, what fraction of each period is the pin HIGH, and what average
                    voltage does the motor see from a 5 V supply?</p></statement>
                </task>
            </activity>
            <slide xml:id="sl-day11x-registers-questions" room="yes">
    <p>With <c>TTmotor_ramp.c</c> open on your laptop, and the reference manual at section 17.4, <em>TIM14 registers</em>, page 482.</p>
    <ol>
        <li>Section 17.3.8 selects PWM mode by writing <c>0110</c> or <c>0111</c> into a field called OC1M. Which register is that field in? Which of the two choices holds the pin HIGH until the counter reaches the compare value, and then drops it? What would the other one have looked like on the scope?</li>
        <li>Find the bit named CC1E, and the register it lives in. If you were to delete the one line of <c>tim14_pa7_pwm_init()</c> that sets this bit, and changed nothing else, what would you see on PA7?</li>
        <li><c>tim14_pa7_pwm_init()</c> writes one more register that neither of those questions asked about, just before it starts the counter. Find it in the file, then look it up in section 17.4. What does the bit our program sets do, and why do it before starting the counter rather than after?</li>
        <li>If you finish: our program calls tim14_pwm_set(625), and the counter runs to 1249. What duty cycle is that, and what average voltage does the motor see from its 5 V supply?</li>
    </ol>
    <note>≈ 8 min on these three. Say at the START that we go through all of them together afterwards, so nobody's fifteen minutes depends on their group being fast. Checkpoint at about minute 7: ask every table to confirm they are actually in section 17.4 — a group in the datasheet, or in the TIM1 chapter, will find nothing and will not say so.</note>
</slide>

            <instructor xml:id="inst-day11x-registers">
                <p><term>The whole mapping.</term>  The three new answers are the last
                three; the rest is the recall table we opened the Part with.</p>
                <p><c>RCC_IOPENR</c> gives GPIOA its clock.  <c>GPIOA_MODER</c> puts PA7 in
                alternate-function mode and <c>GPIOA_AFR[0]</c> selects AF4, which is
                TIM14_CH1.  <c>RCC_APBENR2</c> gives TIM14 its clock.  <c>TIM14_PSC</c> is
                the prescaler and <c>TIM14_ARR</c> the value the counter runs up to.
                <c>TIM14_CR1</c> starts the counter, and <c>TIM14_CCR1</c> is the one we
                write while the motor is running.</p>
                <p><term>PWM mode.</term>  OC1M lives in <c>TIM14_CCMR1</c>, the
                capture/compare mode register.  <c>0110</c> is PWM mode 1: channel 1 is
                active while the counter is below the compare value and inactive once it
                reaches it — HIGH first, then LOW, which is what we want.  <c>0111</c> is
                PWM mode 2, which is the same waveform inverted: the pin would be LOW for
                the first part of every period and HIGH for the rest, so a compare value
                that should have meant slow would have driven the motor fast.  The duty
                cycle would run backwards.</p>
            </instructor>
            <instructor xml:id="inst-day11x-registers-b">
                <p><term>CC1E.</term>  It is bit 0 of <c>TIM14_CCER</c>, the capture/compare
                enable register, and the manual says that with it set the OC1 signal is
                output on the corresponding output pin.  Without it the counter still
                counts, the comparator still compares, and the channel still produces its
                signal internally — but nothing reaches PA7, so the pin stays at whatever
                the output stage leaves it at and the motor never turns.  This is worth
                dwelling on: it is the one line whose absence produces a board that looks
                completely correct and does nothing, and they will run into it again in Lab 6.</p>
                <p>Section 17.4's own wording, which is what the room was sent to find:
                CC1E — <q>Capture mode enabled / OC1 signal is output on the corresponding
                output pin</q>; UG — <q>Re-initialize the counter and generates an update of
                the registers.  Note that the prescaler counter is cleared too.</q></p>
                <p><term>The extra register.</term>  <c>TIM14_EGR</c>, the event generation
                register, and the bit is UG.  Setting it re-initializes the counter and
                updates the registers.  They saw this on Day 10, on the timer we used for
                the display: <c>PSC</c> and <c>ARR</c> are buffered, so the values we write
                do not reach the working copies until an update event happens.  Force one
                before starting the counter and the very first period is already the period
                we asked for; leave it out and the first period runs on whatever the
                prescaler held before, which for a motor is one wrong pulse at startup.</p>
                <p><term>The early-finisher answer.</term>  625 out of 1250 steps is half of
                each period, so a 50% duty cycle and about 2.5 V average on a 5 V supply.
                Accept 625/1249 too — the difference is a tenth of a percent and the point
                is the fraction, not the rounding.</p>
            </instructor>
        </subsection>

        <!-- Part 3 -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day11x-hardware">
            <title>Part 3: Where Those Registers Are Located in the Hardware</title>

            <!-- SHARED FILE: this is Day11-Motors/fig-pwm-scope.png, Day 11's own
                 scope capture, and Day 11x is now its only slide user (Day 11
                 projects the video).  Day 11's Gate 2 left a re-crop of it open
                 with Petra — a re-crop made "for Day 11" would silently change
                 this prediction slide, so re-check sl-day11x-predict-ccr1 if the
                 file is ever replaced. -->
            <figure xml:id="fig-pwm-scope-still">
                <caption>One frozen moment of the trace you watched on Day 11
                (<xref ref="fig-pwm-scope"/>).  The window is about two milliseconds wide, so
                it holds roughly three periods of the 1.6 kHz waveform and the duty cycle
                barely changes across the screen.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day11-Motors/fig-pwm-scope.png" width="92%"/>
            </figure>
            <slide xml:id="sl-day11x-predict-ccr1" ref="fig-pwm-scope-still">
    <ul>
        <li>This is the trace you measured on Day 11, while the ramp was running.</li>
        <li>The width of the HIGH part changed as the motor sped up and slowed down. The period did not.</li>
        <li>So which of the two numbers we set up — the value the counter runs to, or the value it is compared against — is the program rewriting every two seconds?</li>
    </ul>
    <note>≈ 90 s, predict before Figure 176 names it. Answer: the compare value, CCR1. ARR is written once in the init and never touched again. Take a show of hands on each before revealing — the wrong answer is instructive, because a program that rewrote ARR would change the frequency instead of the speed.</note>
</slide>

            <p>
                The width of the HIGH part changed as the motor sped up and slowed down,
                while the period did not.  So which of the two numbers we set up — the
                value the counter runs to, or the value it is compared against — is the
                program rewriting every two seconds?
            </p>

            <p>
                The answer is the compare value.  The program writes a new one every two
                seconds as the ramp climbs and falls, and it never touches the auto-reload
                register after the initialization, which is why the width of the HIGH portion
                moves while the period stays exactly where it is.  A program that rewrote the
                auto-reload value instead would change the frequency of the waveform rather
                than its duty cycle, and the motor would not speed up at all.
                <xref ref="fig-tim14-main-circuit"/> is where both of those numbers are.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-tim14-main-circuit">
                <caption>Where the two numbers actually are (RM0490, Figure 176).  The
                counter runs along the bottom and the compare value is shown above it, and the
                comparator to the right of them is what flips the output partway up each period —
                which is why <c>TIM14_CCR1</c> sets how long the pin stays HIGH and
                <c>TIM14_ARR</c>, the value the counter runs up to, sets how long the period
                is.  The manual calls the working copy of the compare value a
                <term>shadow register</term>; it is the same buffered arrangement we saw
                for the prescaler in the timers chapter
                (<xref ref="subsec-day8-rcw0"/>).</caption>
                <image source="images/Day11x-Motors(2)/fig-tim14-main-circuit.png" width="92%"/>
            </figure>
            <slide xml:id="sl-day11x-main-circuit" ref="fig-tim14-main-circuit">
    <caption>Two registers, two halves of the waveform: one sets how long the pin stays HIGH, the other sets how often that repeats.</caption>
    <note>≈ 3 min. This is the answer to the prediction slide. "Shadow register" is RM0490's word for the buffered copy from Day 8 — say that once, use "buffered" after, and do not let it become a second concept.</note>
</slide>

            <p>
                We already worked out what CC1E does (it lets the channel's output reach
                the pin).  What the figures have not shown yet
                is the output stage the comparator's result travels through on its way to
                PA7 — the enable bit is the last thing it passes through.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-tim14-output-stage">
                <caption>How the signal gets out to the pin (RM0490, Figure 177).  The
                waveform produced by the comparator in <xref ref="fig-tim14-main-circuit"/>
                arrives at the left and reaches the pin at the right only if the output
                enable circuit lets it through, which is what the CC1E bit does.  One
                difference from the printed manual worth knowing: RM0490 labels these boxes
                <c>TIM1_CCER</c>, which is a slip carried over from the chapter on TIM1 —
                the figure is in the TIM14 chapter and the register our code writes is
                TIM14's.  We have put it back to <c>TIMx_CCER</c>, the manual's own way of
                writing <q>whichever timer you are using</q>, which is how the same figure
                already labels <c>TIMx_CCMR1</c> at the lower left, under the OC1M box.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day11x-Motors(2)/fig-tim14-output-stage.png" width="92%"/>
            </figure>
            <slide xml:id="sl-day11x-output-stage" ref="fig-tim14-output-stage">
    <caption>The channel's waveform only reaches PA7 once CC1E is set in TIM14_CCER — leave that line out and the counter runs, the compare register is written, and the pin does nothing.</caption>
    <note>≈ 2 min. If the clock is tight this is NOT the slide to drop — this is the "my pin is silent and everything looks right" bug, and they will hit it in Lab 6 at 11 pm. The TIM1_CCER mislabel is in the caption; mention it only if someone asks.</note>
</slide>

            <p>
                We'll look at those registers once more as section 17.4 actually prints
                them, because that is the form you'll be reading in the lab and in the rest
                of this chapter: a row of numbered bits with a name written across the ones
                that belong to each field.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-tim14-register-map">
                <caption>The timer's four registers as rows of bits, the way section 17.4
                prints them: the counter itself, and the three numbers we set around it.  The
                prescaler sets the rate the counter counts at, the auto-reload register sets
                the period, and the capture/compare register sets the HIGH time.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day11x-Motors(2)/fig-tim14-register-map.png" width="88%"/>
            </figure>
            <slide xml:id="sl-day11x-register-map" ref="fig-tim14-register-map">
    <caption>The prescaler sets the counting rate, the auto-reload register sets the period, and the compare register sets the HIGH time — four rows of bits, all sixteen wide.</caption>
    <note>≈ 2 min, AND THIS IS THE PRESSURE VALVE. If Part 2 has overrun, cut this slide: Figure 176 already annotates ARR and CCR1, so the only thing lost is the prescaler-to-counting-rate link, which Day 8 taught. Do not cut the output-stage slide instead.</note>
</slide>
        </subsection>

        <!-- Part 4 -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day11x-driver">
            <title>Part 4: Reading the Driver</title>
            <p>
                Now we'll work out what value goes into each of those registers.  Section
                17.4 gives every register as a row of bits, and the value our program needs is
                assembled from the position of a field and the pattern that goes in it.  Three of the registers we need are the easy case, because the thing we
                set is a single bit; <c>TIM14_CCMR1</c> is the harder case, because there is
                more than one bitfield we need to set in it, one of which consists of four
                bits not all of which are adjacent to each other.
            </p>

            <!-- P-6: the decode activity comes BEFORE the walkthrough, and
                 the CCMR1 figures are the reveal, so they are placed after the
                 instructor answer rather than above the activity. -->
            <figure xml:id="fig-ccer-cc1e">
                <caption>The capture/compare enable register, <c>TIM14_CCER</c>, bit by bit.
                CC1E is bit 0.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day11x-Motors(2)/fig-ccer-cc1e.svg" width="90%"/>
            </figure>
            <figure xml:id="fig-egr-ug">
                <caption>The event generation register, <c>TIM14_EGR</c>.  UG is bit 0, and
                it is write-only — the hardware clears it again as soon as it has acted on
                it.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day11x-Motors(2)/fig-egr-ug.svg" width="90%"/>
            </figure>
            <figure xml:id="fig-cr1-cen">
                <caption>Control register 1, <c>TIM14_CR1</c>.  CEN is bit 0, and it is the
                switch that starts the counter.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day11x-Motors(2)/fig-cr1-cen.svg" width="90%"/>
            </figure>

            <activity xml:id="act-day11x-bitfields">
                <title>From a register name to a register value</title>
                <introduction>
                    <p>Here are four lines from <c>TTmotor_ramp.c</c>.  <c>ES28.h</c> defines
                    <c>TIM_OC1_PWM1</c> as 6 and <c>TIM_CC1_OUTPUT</c> as 0, and every
                    <c>_Pos</c> name is the bit position of the field it names.  Work each one
                    out on paper before we walk through the file.</p>
                </introduction>
                <task xml:id="task-day11x-decode-oc1m">
                    <statement><p><c>TIM14->CCMR1 |= TIM_OC1_PWM1 &lt;&lt; TIM_CCMR1_OC1M_Pos;</c>
                    — write 6 in binary, and deduce which bits of <c>CCMR1</c> this sets.  Does
                    that put the PWM mode the Reference Manual (Section 17.3.8) asked for into
                    the OC1M field?  The
                    line above it is <c>TIM14->CCMR1 &amp;= ~TIM_CCMR1_OC1M_Msk;</c> — what
                    could go wrong if we left that line out?</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day11x-decode-cc1e">
                    <statement><p><c>TIM14->CCER |= TIM_CCER_CC1E;</c> — find
                    <c>TIM14_CCER</c>, the capture/compare enable register, in Section 17.4 of the
                    Reference Manual, and read which bit CC1E is.  What is the numeric value of
                    <c>TIM_CCER_CC1E</c>?  Why is this line written with <c>|=</c> and no
                    clearing line before it, when the OC1M line above needed
                    one?</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day11x-decode-cen">
                    <statement><p><c>TIM14->CR1 |= TIM_CR1_CEN;</c> — find
                    <c>TIM14_CR1</c>, control register 1, in the same section, and read which
                    bit CEN is.  What value is that, and what happens on the pin the instant
                    this line runs?</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day11x-decode-cc1s">
                    <statement><p><c>TIM14->CCMR1 |= TIM_CC1_OUTPUT &lt;&lt; TIM_CCMR1_CC1S_Pos;</c>
                    — the same register as the first question, and a second field of it.
                    Which bits is CC1S, and what does <c>00</c> select?  The comment in
                    <c>TTmotor_ramp.c</c> calls it the default value — so why write it at
                    all?</p></statement>
                </task>
            </activity>
            <instructor xml:id="inst-day11x-bitfields">
                <p><term>OC1M.</term>  6 is <c>0110</c>, which is exactly the PWM mode 1
                pattern section 17.3.8 asked for, shifted up to where the OC1M field starts.
                The clearing line matters because <c>|=</c> can only turn bits on: if OC1M
                already held, say, <c>0011</c>, then OR-ing <c>0110</c> into it gives
                <c>0111</c> — PWM mode 2, the inverted waveform.  Clear the field first, then
                set it.  This is the read-modify-write pattern from the very first chapter,
                and it is why the driver has two lines where one would seem to do.</p>
                <p><term>CC1E.</term>  Bit 0, so the value is 1.  No clearing line is needed
                because this is a single bit, not a multi-bit field: OR-ing 1 into a bit that
                is already 1 leaves it 1.  A field needs clearing, a flag does not.</p>
                <p><term>CEN.</term>  Bit 0 of CR1, so again 1.  The counter starts counting
                immediately, and because <c>CCR1</c> was set to 0 in the line above, the
                compare value is 0 and the pin stays LOW — the motor does not move until the
                program writes a speed.  That is deliberate, and it is why
                <c>TIM14->CCR1 = 0;</c> is in the initialization at all: it is the one line
                in the function that is there for safety rather than for
                configuration.</p>
                <p><term>CC1S.</term>  Bits 1:0 of <c>CCMR1</c>, and <c>00</c> configures
                the channel as an output.  It is also the reset value, so on a chip that has
                just powered up these two lines change nothing — which is the interesting
                part.  A driver does not get to assume what ran before it: after a soft reset,
                or after a program that had used the channel as an input capture, CC1S would
                not be <c>00</c> and the pin would never drive.  It is also why <c>CCMR1</c>
                is written twice, which is the question the checkpoint ends on: two of its
                fields are ours.</p>
            </instructor>

            <p>
                <c>TIM14_CCMR1</c>, the capture/compare mode register, is different, because
                neither of the things we set in it is a single bit.  OC1M, which chooses what the channel does, is four bits wide
                and split across the register — three of them together and a fourth up at bit
                16.  CC1S, which decides whether the channel is an output or an input, is two
                bits at the bottom.  A field several bits wide cannot be reliably set by
                OR-ing a value into it, because OR can only turn bits on: if the field
                already holds a 1 where the new pattern wants a 0, that bit stays set, and the
                result is the two patterns mixed together.  That is why each of
                these two gets a clearing line before its setting line, and why the three
                single-bit registers do not.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-ccmr1-oc1m-bits">
                <caption><c>TIM14_CCMR1</c> laid out bit by bit, with the first of our two
                fields marked.  OC1M selects what the channel does, and it is four bits wide
                and not in one piece: three of them at 6:4, and the fourth up at bit 16.  Four
                bits is what makes it different from CC1E, CEN and UG, which are one bit
                each.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day11x-Motors(2)/fig-ccmr1-oc1m.png" width="96%"/>
            </figure>
            <figure xml:id="fig-ccmr1-cc1s-bits">
                <caption>The same register, with our other field marked.  CC1S is two bits at
                1:0, and it decides whether the channel is an output or an input.  Two fields
                in one register is why the initialization writes <c>CCMR1</c> twice.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day11x-Motors(2)/fig-ccmr1-cc1s.png" width="96%"/>
            </figure>
            <slide xml:id="sl-day11x-bitfield-registers">
    <p>Two of your four answers, as section 17.4 prints them: the register CC1E lives in, and the register CEN lives in. The other two are both <c>CCMR1</c>, on the next two slides.</p>
    <image source="images/Day11x-Motors(2)/fig-ccer-cc1e.svg" width="100%"/>
    <image source="images/Day11x-Motors(2)/fig-cr1-cen.svg" width="100%"/>
    <caption>CC1E is bit 0 of CCER, CEN is bit 0 of CR1. One bit each — which is why <c>|=</c> on its own is safe here, and why the two CCMR1 fields are not.</caption>
    <note>≈ 1 min, and it runs straight into the answer slide as ONE beat of two minutes, not two beats. Walk the answers here, with the registers on the wall, and let the answer slide be the reveal in writing rather than a second telling. The contrast to draw is with CCMR1, which follows: these two are single bits, so OR-ing 1 in is safe; OC1M and CC1S are multi-bit fields, so they have to be cleared first. EGR is the same easy case as these two and was answered in Part 2 — its bit map is in the book, and it does not need the wall again. That is the answer to "why does one need a clearing line and the others do not".</note>
</slide>

            <figure xml:id="fig-ccmr1-oc1m">
                <caption>Everything our program does to <c>TIM14_CCMR1</c>, in one place: the
                bit map at the top, then section 17.4's description of each field with the
                value we write boxed, and beside it the line that writes it.  In blue, OC1M
                gets <c>0110</c> — PWM mode 1, which the manual defines as channel 1 being
                active as long as the counter is below the compare value.  In magenta, CC1S
                gets <c>00</c>, which is what keeps the channel an output.  Two fields, one
                register, which is why the initialization writes <c>CCMR1</c> twice.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day11x-Motors(2)/fig-ccmr1-oc1m_cc1s.png" width="92%"/>
            </figure>
            <slide xml:id="sl-day11x-ccmr1-oc1m" ref="fig-ccmr1-oc1m-bits" stack="yes">
    <ul>
        <li>OC1M is four bits, and they are not together: 6:4, and a fourth at bit 16.</li>
        <li>Section 17.3.8 of the Reference Manual on <c>0110</c>: <q>PWM mode 1 — Channel 1 is active as long as TIMx_CNT &lt; TIMx_CCR1 else inactive.</q> HIGH until the counter reaches the compare value, then LOW.</li>
        <li>Four bits is why the line needs a partner: <c>CCMR1 &amp;= ~TIM_CCMR1_OC1M_Msk;</c> first, then <c>CCMR1 |= TIM_OC1_PWM1 &lt;&lt; TIM_CCMR1_OC1M_Pos;</c></li>
    </ul>
    <caption>Clear the field, then set it — because OR alone would leave whatever was there and mix the two patterns together.</caption>
    <note>≈ 1 min, and it is the reveal for decode task (a) — the first of the two CCMR1 fields — do not project it before they have written something down. The full page from the manual, with these words in place, is the figure in their book.</note>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day11x-ccmr1-cc1s" ref="fig-ccmr1-cc1s-bits" stack="yes">
    <ul>
        <li>CC1S is two bits at 1:0.</li>
        <li>Section 17.4 of the Reference Manual on <c>00</c>: <q>CC1 channel is configured as output.</q> That is what keeps the channel an output, and it is what the second <c>CCMR1</c> pair writes.</li>
        <li>OC1M and CC1S are two different fields in the same register — which is why the initialization writes <c>CCMR1</c> twice.</li>
    </ul>
    <caption>The answer to the checkpoint question that comes later: CCMR1 is the register written twice, because two of its fields are ours.</caption>
    <note>≈ 1 min, and it is the reveal for decode task (d), which is the only place CC1S is asked about before it appears here. <c>00</c> is also the reset value, so this pair changes nothing on a fresh chip — say that, and say why it is written anyway: the driver does not get to assume what ran before it.</note>
</slide>

            <p>
                That is every register the initialization touches, so we can now read it
                straight through.  The function runs in three stretches: it sets up the GPIO
                pin in the mode the timer needs, it sets the timer's own numbers, and it puts
                the channel into PWM mode and starts it.  Nothing in it is new at this point, which is the whole
                idea — by the time you reach the last line every register in it should be one
                you have already looked up.
            </p>
            <p>
                The pin first.  These are the same three operations we used on Day 11 —
                give the port a clock, put the pin in alternate-function mode, and select
                which alternate function.
            </p>
            <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
void tim14_pa7_pwm_init(void) {
	// Enable clock access to GPIOA
	RCC->IOPENR |=  RCC_IOPENR_GPIOAEN;

	// Set PA7 in alternate function mode (10)
	GPIOA->MODER &= ~GPIO_MODER_MODE7_Msk;
	GPIOA->MODER |= GPIO_ALTERNATE << GPIO_MODER_MODE7_Pos;

	// Set PA7 alternate function type (AF4, 0100)
	GPIOA->AFR[0] &= ~GPIO_AFRL_AFSEL7_Msk;
	GPIOA->AFR[0] |= GPIO_AF4 << GPIO_AFRL_AFSEL7_Pos;
]]></code></program>
            <p>
                Then come the timer's own numbers.  <c>PSC_FACTOR</c> and <c>PWM_TIMER_MAX</c> are
                the two constants at the top of the file, 6 and 1250, and both are written
                one less than the value we have in mind because the counter starts at zero.
                The last of these four lines is the only one in the function that is there
                for safety rather than for configuration: <c>TIM14->CCR1 = 0;</c> sets the
                starting speed to zero, so the motor cannot jump the moment the counter
                starts.
            </p>
            <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
	// Enable clock access to Timer 14
	RCC->APBENR2 |= RCC_APBENR2_TIM14EN;

	// Set prescaler
	TIM14->PSC = PSC_FACTOR-1;

	// Set autoreload value
	TIM14->ARR = PWM_TIMER_MAX-1;

	// Set the compare register value - start with a speed of 0
	TIM14->CCR1 = 0;
]]></code></program>
            <p>
                This is the part that makes it a PWM channel.  The two <c>CCMR1</c> pairs are the
                read-modify-write we decoded above; <c>CC1E</c> lets the channel reach the
                pin; <c>UG</c> forces the update event that loads the buffered values; and
                <c>CEN</c> starts the counter.
            </p>
            <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
	// Set pwm mode 1 (0110): pin is high up to capture value, then goes low
	TIM14->CCMR1 &= ~TIM_CCMR1_OC1M_Msk;
	TIM14->CCMR1 |= TIM_OC1_PWM1 << TIM_CCMR1_OC1M_Pos;

	// Make sure timer is in output mode; this is default value
	TIM14->CCMR1 &= ~TIM_CCMR1_CC1S_Msk;
	TIM14->CCMR1 |= TIM_CC1_OUTPUT << TIM_CCMR1_CC1S_Pos;

	// Enable TIM14_CH1 in output mode
	TIM14->CCER |= TIM_CCER_CC1E;

	// Generate an update event - this clears counter, prescaler counter, updates registers
	TIM14->EGR |= TIM_EGR_UG;

	// Enable timer
	TIM14->CR1 |= TIM_CR1_CEN;
}
]]></code></program>
            <p>
                Here is the function that controls the motor speed while it is running: the
                clamp keeps the value inside the range the counter can represent, and then one
                assignment sets the speed.  The <c>value &lt; 0</c> test is technically not
                necessary here since a <c>uint16_t</c> value will never be less than zero, but
                it will help ensure you never set the compare register to a value outside the
                correct range by accident: when you write <c>motor_speed()</c> for Lab 6,
                taking a signed parameter is the natural choice, and then that line is all
                that stands between a negative speed and a compare value of sixty-five
                thousand.
            </p>
            <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
void tim14_pwm_set(uint16_t value) {
// Safety first: Allow no pulse values outside the (0, PWM_TIMER_MAX-1 range!
	if (value>PWM_TIMER_MAX-1)
		value = PWM_TIMER_MAX-1;
	else if (value < 0)
		value = 0;
	TIM14->CCR1 = value;
}
]]></code></program>
            <!-- ONE BEAT, TWO SLIDES.  Gate 3 asked for these two to be merged
                 into a single slide (item 21, three reviewers).  Merged they do
                 not fit: side by side at 47% the two listings clip 149 px and
                 99 px across and the body runs 81 px past the slide, measured in
                 the player at 1600x900, and the code lines that clip are the
                 driver's own, so B-6 leaves no lever.  The minute Gate 3 wanted
                 is taken here instead — three minutes down to two, with the pin
                 half narrated rather than read out. -->
            <slide xml:id="sl-day11x-init-pin">
    <p>We now have every register we need, so here is the whole initialization, in the order it runs. We'll take it in three pieces — and the first is getting the pin ready for PWM mode, which is where Day 11 left off.</p>
    <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
void tim14_pa7_pwm_init(void) {
	// Enable clock access to GPIOA
	RCC->IOPENR |=  RCC_IOPENR_GPIOAEN;

	// Set PA7 in alternate function mode (10)
	GPIOA->MODER &= ~GPIO_MODER_MODE7_Msk;
	GPIOA->MODER |= GPIO_ALTERNATE << GPIO_MODER_MODE7_Pos;

	// Set PA7 alternate function type (AF4, 0100)
	GPIOA->AFR[0] &= ~GPIO_AFRL_AFSEL7_Msk;
	GPIOA->AFR[0] |= GPIO_AF4 << GPIO_AFRL_AFSEL7_Pos;
]]></code></program>
    <caption>Clock, mode, function — the same three steps as every pin we have configured.</caption>
    <note>≈ 30 s, and this slide and the next are ONE beat: do not stop on this one. It is Day 11 recall — they wrote MODER and AFR themselves in act-day11-pwm, and the file is open on their laptops — so name the three steps and move on. The time belongs to the next slide.</note>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day11x-init-timing">
    <p>Second piece: getting the timer to count at the correct rate, and initializing the compare register value. Two of the numbers come from constants set at the top of the file.</p>
    <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
#define PSC_FACTOR	6		    // 12 MHz / 6 = 2 MHz
#define PWM_TIMER_MAX	  1250		// 2 MHz / 1250 = 1.6 kHz with prescale 6
]]></code></program>
    <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
	// Enable clock access to Timer 14
	RCC->APBENR2 |= RCC_APBENR2_TIM14EN;

	// Set prescaler
	TIM14->PSC = PSC_FACTOR-1;

	// Set autoreload value
	TIM14->ARR = PWM_TIMER_MAX-1;

	// Set the compare register value - start with a speed of 0
	TIM14->CCR1 = 0;
]]></code></program>
    <caption>Rate, range — and a starting speed of zero, so the motor cannot jump when the counter starts.</caption>
    <note>≈ 90 s, the second half of the same beat. The first three lines are Day 8, and the arithmetic behind PSC_FACTOR-1 and PWM_TIMER_MAX-1 is what they did on Day 11. CCR1 = 0 is the one that is neither: it is the safety line, and it is the only line here worth dwelling on.</note>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day11x-init-mode">
    <p>Last piece: what makes it a PWM channel rather than a plain counter.</p>
    <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
	// Set pwm mode 1 (0110): pin is high up to capture value, then goes low
	TIM14->CCMR1 &= ~TIM_CCMR1_OC1M_Msk;
	TIM14->CCMR1 |= TIM_OC1_PWM1 << TIM_CCMR1_OC1M_Pos;

	// Make sure timer is in output mode; this is default value
	TIM14->CCMR1 &= ~TIM_CCMR1_CC1S_Msk;
	TIM14->CCMR1 |= TIM_CC1_OUTPUT << TIM_CCMR1_CC1S_Pos;

	// Enable TIM14_CH1 in output mode
	TIM14->CCER |= TIM_CCER_CC1E;

	// Generate an update event - this clears counter, prescaler counter, updates registers
	TIM14->EGR |= TIM_EGR_UG;

	// Enable timer
	TIM14->CR1 |= TIM_CR1_CEN;
}
]]></code></program>
    <caption>PWM mode, the output enable, the update event, and the line that starts the counter — every register in them is one you just looked up.</caption>
    <note>≈ 3 min, one beat per block. The two CCMR1 pairs are the read-modify-write from the decode activity: clear the field, then set it. EGR/UG is Day 10 recall — "the buffered registers from the display timer" — and CC1E is the line whose absence gives a silent pin.</note>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day11x-set-code">
    <p>And the only thing that changes while the motor is running.</p>
    <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
void tim14_pwm_set(uint16_t value) {
// Safety first: Allow no pulse values outside the (0, PWM_TIMER_MAX-1 range!
	if (value>PWM_TIMER_MAX-1)
		value = PWM_TIMER_MAX-1;
	else if (value < 0)
		value = 0;
	TIM14->CCR1 = value;
}
]]></code></program>
    <caption>Only the compare register changes while the motor turns — and one count of it is 500 ns of pulse width, which is the finest speed step this channel has.</caption>
    <note>≈ 2 min, and this is the day's closing idea — set it up as such. Expect someone to notice that value &lt; 0 can never be true for a uint16_t, and they are right: as written, that branch cannot run. Keep it, and say why. The guard is there for the SIGNATURE, not for this one call. In Lab 6 they write motor_speed() themselves, and taking an int16_t is the natural choice — the ramp counter is signed, and abs() is easy to forget. The moment the parameter is signed, that branch is the only thing between a negative speed and a compare value of 65,000-odd, which is a motor at full tilt rather than stopped. Defensive code earns its place by surviving a change of type; this is a good half-minute on why you leave it in.</note>
</slide>

            <p>
                Once that function has run, the motor's speed is changed by writing one
                register and nothing else.  <c>tim14_pwm_set()</c> limits the value it is
                given to the range the counter can represent and then assigns it to
                <c>CCR1</c>; the prescaler, the auto-reload value, the mode bits and the
                output enable are all left exactly as the initialization set them, and the
                timer keeps producing the waveform without any further involvement from the
                CPU.
            </p>
            <activity xml:id="act-day11x-checkpoint">
                <title>Name the register</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>On your own, without looking back.  Each step of the initialization
                    does one of these jobs.  Write in the register it modifies.</p>
                    <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="major" left="minor" right="minor">
                        <col halign="left" width="55%"/>
                        <col halign="center" width="45%"/>
                        <row bottom="major">
                            <cell>The line does this</cell>
                            <cell>so it writes this register</cell>
                        </row>
                        <row bottom="minor"><cell>Give the pin's port a clock</cell><cell></cell></row>
                        <row bottom="minor"><cell>Put PA7 in alternate-function mode</cell><cell></cell></row>
                        <row bottom="minor"><cell>Select which alternate function</cell><cell></cell></row>
                        <row bottom="minor"><cell>Give the timer a clock</cell><cell></cell></row>
                        <row bottom="minor"><cell>Set the counting rate</cell><cell></cell></row>
                        <row bottom="minor"><cell>Set the value the counter runs up to</cell><cell></cell></row>
                        <row bottom="minor"><cell>Set the starting speed</cell><cell></cell></row>
                        <row bottom="minor"><cell>Select PWM mode</cell><cell></cell></row>
                        <row bottom="minor"><cell>Keep the channel an output</cell><cell></cell></row>
                        <row bottom="minor"><cell>Let the channel reach the pin</cell><cell></cell></row>
                        <row bottom="minor"><cell>Force an update event</cell><cell></cell></row>
                        <row><cell>Start the counter</cell><cell></cell></row>
                    </tabular>
                    <p>Twelve steps, but not twelve different registers.  Which one is
                    written twice, and why?</p>
                </statement>
            </activity>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day11x-checkpoint">
    <p>On your own, without looking back. Each step of the initialization does one of these jobs — write in the register it writes.</p>
    <sidebyside widths="66%" margins="17%">
        <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="major" left="minor" right="minor">
            <col halign="left" width="55%"/>
            <col halign="center" width="45%"/>
            <row bottom="major">
                <cell>The line does this</cell>
                <cell>so it writes this register</cell>
            </row>
            <row bottom="minor"><cell>Give the pin's port a clock</cell><cell></cell></row>
            <row bottom="minor"><cell>Put PA7 in alternate-function mode</cell><cell></cell></row>
            <row bottom="minor"><cell>Select which alternate function</cell><cell></cell></row>
            <row bottom="minor"><cell>Give the timer a clock</cell><cell></cell></row>
            <row bottom="minor"><cell>Set the counting rate</cell><cell></cell></row>
            <row bottom="minor"><cell>Set the value the counter runs up to</cell><cell></cell></row>
            <row bottom="minor"><cell>Set the starting speed</cell><cell></cell></row>
            <row bottom="minor"><cell>Select PWM mode</cell><cell></cell></row>
            <row bottom="minor"><cell>Keep the channel an output</cell><cell></cell></row>
            <row bottom="minor"><cell>Let the channel reach the pin</cell><cell></cell></row>
            <row bottom="minor"><cell>Force an update event</cell><cell></cell></row>
            <row><cell>Start the counter</cell><cell></cell></row>
        </tabular>
    </sidebyside>
    <caption>Twelve steps, but not twelve different registers. Which one is written twice, and why?</caption>
    <note>≈ 90 s, individual, and protect it — it is the only moment all hour a student produces the mapping unaided. Then read the answers off the next slide.</note>
</slide>
            <instructor xml:id="inst-day11x-checkpoint">
                <p>Down the column: <c>RCC_IOPENR</c>, <c>GPIOA_MODER</c>,
                <c>GPIOA_AFR[0]</c>, <c>RCC_APBENR2</c>, <c>TIM14_PSC</c>, <c>TIM14_ARR</c>,
                <c>TIM14_CCR1</c>, <c>TIM14_CCMR1</c>, <c>TIM14_CCMR1</c> again,
                <c>TIM14_CCER</c>, <c>TIM14_EGR</c>, <c>TIM14_CR1</c>.</p>
                <p>The one written twice is <c>TIM14_CCMR1</c> — once for the OC1M field to
                select PWM mode, and once for CC1S to keep the channel as an output.  Both
                fields live in the same register, so the twelve steps need only eleven
                registers, written over sixteen lines.</p>
                <p>Ninety seconds, then read the answers out.  This is the day's crucial
                step, and it is the only moment a student produces the mapping without help
                — so give it the ninety seconds even if Part 4 is running long.  Cut the
                brake-and-<c>abs</c> discussion instead.</p>
            </instructor>

            <p>
                In Lab 6 you are asked to turn this into a device driver of your own, with an
                initialization function, a mode-select function and a speed-select function
                in a file called <c>tb6612.c</c>.  Two of the three are almost in front of
                you already: <c>tim14_pa7_pwm_init()</c> becomes the initialization once you
                fold in the two lines that make PA5 and PA6 outputs, and
                <c>tim14_pwm_set()</c> becomes the speed-select nearly unchanged.
            </p>
            <slide xml:id="sl-day11x-lab6-seam">
    <ul>
        <li>In Lab 6 you are asked to write three functions: <c>motor_init()</c>, <c>motor_mode()</c> and <c>motor_speed()</c>.</li>
        <li><c>tim14_pa7_pwm_init()</c> is most of <c>motor_init()</c> — add the two lines in <c>main()</c> that make PA5 and PA6 outputs, and keep <c>TIM14->CCR1 = 0;</c>.</li>
        <li><c>tim14_pwm_set()</c> is <c>motor_speed()</c> almost unchanged.</li>
        <li><c>motor_mode()</c> is the truth table from Day 11 — IN1 and IN2 for forward, reverse, brake and stop.</li>
        <li>What is <em>not</em> <c>motor_mode()</c>: the direction logic in <c>main()</c>. That code decides <em>when</em> to brake as the ramp turns around, which is your program's job, not the driver's.</li>
    </ul>
    <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
	// in main(), as the ramp turns around:
	if (counter_old < 0) {
		TB6612_AIN1 = 1;  TB6612_AIN2 = 1;   // short brake
	} else {
		TB6612_AIN1 = 1;  TB6612_AIN2 = 0;   // CW
	}
	TB6612_PWMA = abs(counter_new);              // magnitude only
]]></code></program>
    <caption>The driver contains the code to move the motor; your program calls driver functions to create a certain behavior.</caption>
    <note>≈ 2 min, and the bullets carry the answer — read the seam off the slide rather than discussing it open-ended; this is the Part's other minute back. It is the seam that matters for Lab 6 and it is easy to get wrong: a student who writes motor_mode() from main()'s if-else will drag the ramp's brake-before-reverse decision into the driver, and then cannot call it for a plain forward. Note the driver never produces STOP at all — that case is theirs to add, which is the stretch.</note>
</slide>

            <activity xml:id="act-day11x-stretch">
                <title>Going further</title>
                <introduction>
                    <p>These are optional, for when you have finished the rest.</p>
                </introduction>
                <task xml:id="task-day11x-stretch-mode">
                    <statement><p>Write the body of <c>motor_mode()</c>.  The signature is given in
                    Lab 6 — <c>void motor_mode(uint8_t mode);</c>, with the four
                    modes FWD, REV, BRAKE and STOP — and the TB6612 truth table
                    from Day 11, on page 4 of the driver's datasheet, gives you IN1 and IN2
                    for each.
                    Note that <c>TTmotor_ramp.c</c> only ever produces three of the four: it
                    never puts the driver in stop.  What do IN1 and IN2 have to be for that
                    fourth case, and how is what the motor does then different from
                    brake?</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day11x-stretch-50hz">
                    <statement><p>On Day 11 you predicted what would change if this motor
                    ran at 50 Hz instead of 1.6 kHz.  Now work out the numbers: from a
                    12 MHz clock, what does the prescaler have to divide by, and what does
                    the counter have to run up to, to give a 50 Hz PWM with 2000 steps of
                    duty cycle?  The two commented-out <c>#define</c> lines just below the
                    active ones in <c>TTmotor_ramp.c</c> are that setting — check them
                    against your answer, then try them and listen to the
                    difference.</p></statement>
                </task>
            </activity>
            <instructor xml:id="inst-day11x-stretch">
                <p><term>The stop case.</term>  Both IN1 and IN2 LOW puts the driver in
                stop, and both HIGH is the short brake.  The difference is what the motor's
                terminals are connected to: in brake they are shorted together, so the
                spinning motor drives a current through itself and is dragged to a halt; in
                stop they are left open, so nothing carries the current and the shaft coasts
                to a stop on its own.  The ramp never needs stop because it is always either
                driving or deliberately braking — but a lab that wants a motor to freewheel
                needs it.</p>
                <p><term>The 50 Hz numbers.</term>  12 MHz ÷ 50 Hz = 240,000 counts per
                period.  Split across 2000 steps that is a prescale of 120: 12 MHz ÷ 120 =
                100 kHz, and 100 kHz ÷ 2000 = 50 Hz.  So <c>PSC_FACTOR</c> would have to be
                120 and <c>PWM_TIMER_MAX</c> 2000.</p>
                <p>The commented-out pair in the file is exactly that setting, so a group
                that has done the arithmetic can uncomment the two lines, rebuild, and hear
                the difference — which is the optional experiment in Lab 6.  What they
                should hear at 50 Hz is the pulsing Day 11 asked them to predict, and
                possibly better low-speed torque.</p>
            </instructor>
        </subsection>

        <!-- Part 5 -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day11x-resolution">
            <title>Part 5: Resolution, and What Comes Next</title>
            <p>
                One number matters that the program never states outright: the channel's
                <term>resolution</term>, the smallest change in pulse width we can ask for.
                The counter divides each period into a fixed number of steps and the compare
                register holds whole numbers, so the duty cycle moves in steps rather than
                continuously.  Our period is 625 µs and the counter divides it into 1250
                steps, so one step of <c>CCR1</c> is 625 µs ÷ 1250 = 500 ns of pulse width,
                and 5 V ÷ 1250 = 4 mV of average voltage at the motor.  That is what decides
                whether two speeds you ask for are actually
                different, and it is what your Lab 6 screenshot has to be able to show.
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-pwm-resolution">
                <caption>The narrowest pulse this program can ask for.  The period is fixed
                by the value the counter runs up to, and one count of the compare register
                is one step of pulse width, <m>T_0</m> — so the number of steps sets how
                finely the speed can be adjusted.  That is the channel's
                <term>resolution</term>.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day11x-Motors(2)/fig-pwm-resolution.svg" width="100%"/>
            </figure>
            <slide xml:id="sl-day11x-resolution" ref="fig-pwm-resolution" room="yes">
    <ul>
        <li>Our period is 625 µs, and the counter divides it into 1250 steps.</li>
        <li>So how much time is one step of the compare register? That number is the channel's <term>resolution</term>.</li>
    </ul>
    <note>≈ 2 min, predict then reveal. T_0 = 625 µs / 1250 = 500 ns. They already have the voltage side from the Day 11 stretch — 5 V / 1250 = 4 mV per step — so do NOT re-derive that; state it and join the two: one count of CCR1 is 500 ns of pulse width and 4 mV of average voltage. That is the resolution their Waveforms screenshot for Lab 6 Deliverable 1 has to be able to show.</note>
</slide>

            <p>
                Now that we know how to control the motor's speed along with all of the
                registers needed to do so, we'll turn to measuring the motor's speed to find
                out whether the motor is actually turning as fast as we asked for (under load
                it may not).  The hardware we'll use for measuring speed (a slotted wheel
                sensor) is already mounted on your motor.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-encoder-wheel">
                <caption>Measuring motor speed with a slotted wheel on the shaft,
                and a photointerrupter that sees the light through it interrupted once per
                slot.  Counting those pulses is how the display in the background knows the
                shaft is turning at 222 rpm.  The wheel and the sensor are already part of
                the motor assembly in your kit.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day11x-Motors(2)/fig-encoder-wheel.jpg" width="62%"/>
            </figure>
            <figure xml:id="fig-photointerrupter-video">
                <caption>The photointerrupter's output on the oscilloscope while the motor
                speeds up from about 30 rpm to about 180 rpm.  The pulses are the beam being
                interrupted by each slot in turn, and as the shaft turns faster they crowd
                together — which is the whole idea behind measuring speed this way: the rate
                the pulses arrive at is proportional to the speed of the shaft.</caption>
                <video youtube="VH0-zO2LpDc" width="80%"/>
            </figure>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day11x-next" ref="fig-encoder-wheel">
    <ul>
        <li>We can set the speed. We cannot yet measure it.</li>
        <li>We'll use an <term>optical incremental sensor</term>: a slotted wheel on the shaft, and a photointerrupter whose beam is interrupted once per slot, turning rotation into a train of pulses.</li>
        <li>This is already built into your motor setup — look at it before Thursday.</li>
        <li>The rate those pulses arrive at is proportional to how fast the shaft is turning.</li>
    </ul>
    <note>≈ 3 min. Her slide 18's note is the fact that matters: the sensor is already on their motor. Say it explicitly or they will not look. Thursday is Day 12 — pulses to rpm, the seven-segment display, and the potentiometer as a throttle.</note>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day11x-scope-video">
    <p>The same sensor's output, while the motor speeds up from about 30 rpm to about 180 rpm.</p>
    <video youtube="VH0-zO2LpDc" width="74%"/>
    <caption>The pulses crowd together as the shaft turns faster. On Thursday we'll count them, turn them into rpm, and put the number on the seven-segment display.</caption>
    <note>≈ 1 min. Play it, do not narrate over it. If it will not load within about ten seconds, describe the trace instead and move on — the point survives without the video. This is her own capture from the Day 11x deck; the point to draw out of them afterwards is that the pulse RATE is what carries the speed, not the pulse shape.</note>
</slide>
        </subsection>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <section xml:id="sec-speed-before-class">
        <title>Before Class: Counting Pulses to Measure Speed</title>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                On Wednesday we set the motor's speed, but we could not yet
                measure it.  At the end of class we looked at the sensor that will
                let us: an <term>optical incremental sensor</term>, a slotted wheel
                on the motor's shaft and a photointerrupter whose beam is
                interrupted once per slot (<xref ref="fig-encoder-wheel"/>).  It is
                already mounted on the motor in your kit.  What comes out of it is a
                train of pulses, and the rate those pulses arrive at is proportional
                to how fast the shaft is turning.  In this reading we'll work out how
                to turn that rate into the speed of the shaft in revolutions per
                minute (rpm) — the number we'll count out, convert, and put on the
                seven-segment display in class.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-speed-sensor">
            <title>How the Photointerrupter Turns Rotation Into Pulses</title>
            <p>
                The optical incremental sensor we saw at the end of Wednesday's class has
                two parts.  The first is the slotted wheel, which is fixed to the
                motor's shaft and turns with it.  The second is the photointerrupter,
                the U-shaped part wrapped around the rim of that wheel.  There is an
                LED (light-emitting diode) on one side of the U and a
                <term>phototransistor</term> on the other.  A phototransistor is a
                transistor that is switched by light falling on it rather than by a
                current we supply.  The LED shines steadily across the gap between
                the two sides, and the rim of the wheel passes through that gap
                (<xref ref="fig-photointerrupter-beam"/>).
            </p>
            <p>
                Every time a slot comes around, the light gets through to the
                phototransistor; every time the solid part of the rim between two
                slots comes around, the light is blocked.  So the sensor's output
                switches back and forth once for every slot that goes past, and a
                wheel that is turning steadily produces a steady square wave.
            </p>
            <p>
                For now the two ideas to hold onto are that the <em>number</em> of
                pulses tells us how far the shaft has turned, and that the
                <em>rate</em> at which they arrive is proportional to how fast it is
                turning.
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-photointerrupter-beam">
                <caption>The two parts of the optical incremental sensor, and the pulse
                train they produce.  The slotted wheel turns on the motor's shaft, and
                its rim passes through the gap in the photointerrupter, where an LED
                on one side shines across to a phototransistor on the other.  A slot
                lets the light through and the solid rim between two slots blocks it,
                so the sensor's output switches once for every slot that goes past —
                which makes the rate of those pulses proportional to the speed of the
                shaft.  The wheel in your kit has slots cut all the way around its
                rim, and in class we'll start by counting how many there are.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day12-Motors(3)/fig-photointerrupter-beam.svg" width="92%"/>
            </figure>

            <p>
                The photointerrupter has three connections: power, ground, and an
                output.  The output needs a <term>pull-up resistor</term> — a
                resistor of about 10 kΩ between the output and the supply rail — and
                the same resistor appears in the Lab 6 wiring.  We'll wire it in
                class, put the oscilloscope on the output, and work out there why the
                pull-up is needed.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-speed-rpm">
            <title>From Pulses per Second to Revolutions per Minute</title>
            <p>
                The slotted wheel has some number of slots cut around its rim, and
                we'll write that number <m>N</m>.  One full turn of the shaft carries
                all <m>N</m> of them past the photointerrupter, so one revolution
                produces <m>N</m> pulses.  Everything else is a matter of units.
            </p>
            <p>
                Suppose we count the pulses for exactly one second and call that
                count <term>PPS</term>, for pulses per second.  Dividing by <m>N</m>
                turns pulses into revolutions, so the shaft turned <m>PPS/N</m> times
                during that second.  A minute is sixty seconds, and at a steady speed
                the shaft turns sixty times as far in a minute as it does in a
                second, which gives us its speed in revolutions per minute:
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-rpm-formula">
                <caption>The speed of the shaft in revolutions per minute, from a
                count of pulses taken over one second.  <m>PPS</m> is the number of
                pulses counted in that second and <m>N</m> is the number of slots in
                the wheel.  Dividing by <m>N</m> converts pulses into revolutions,
                and multiplying by 60 converts a rate per second into a rate per
                minute.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day12-Motors(3)/rpm-formula.svg" width="42%"/>
            </figure>

            <p>
                For the wheel in your kit <m>N</m> is 20, which is the number the
                expression in Lab 6 uses, and that makes the arithmetic easy enough
                to do in your head: <m>RPM = 3 \times PPS</m>.  So a count of 60
                pulses in one second means the shaft is turning at 180 rpm.  In class
                we'll count the slots on our own wheels and check that 20 is right,
                because the number in the denominator is the one thing in this
                expression that comes from the hardware rather than from the
                arithmetic.
            </p>
            <p>
                Every pulse looks like every other pulse: the phototransistor is
                either seeing the LED or it is not, and one slot is the same as the
                next.  So does anything about this signal change if the shaft turns
                the other way?  In Lab 6 you are asked to display a negative rpm when
                the motor is running in reverse, so it is a question we have to
                answer, and we'll answer it in class.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <reading-questions xml:id="rq-speed-sensing">
            <exercise xml:id="rq-speed-derivation">
                <statement><p>The slotted wheel on your motor has 20 slots.  You
                count 40 pulses from the photointerrupter in one second.  How fast is
                the shaft turning?</p></statement>
                <choices randomize="yes">
                    <choice correct="yes">
                        <statement><p>120 rpm.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>Correct.  Forty pulses at 20 slots per
                        revolution is two revolutions in that second, and two
                        revolutions per second is 120 per minute — which is
                        <m>60 \times 40 / 20</m>.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>2400 rpm.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>This is <m>60 \times 40</m>, which converts the
                        pulse rate from per-second to per-minute but never converts
                        pulses into revolutions.  Twenty of those pulses make one
                        revolution, so the count has to be divided by 20 as
                        well.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>800 rpm.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>This multiplies by the slot count instead of
                        dividing by it.  More slots on the wheel means more pulses
                        for the same rotation, so a larger slot count makes the speed
                        that a given pulse rate corresponds to
                        <em>smaller</em>.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>40 rpm.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>Forty is the pulse count, not a speed.  It has
                        to be converted twice: pulses into revolutions by dividing by
                        the slot count, and seconds into minutes by multiplying by
                        60.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                </choices>
            </exercise>

            <exercise xml:id="rq-speed-what-carries-it">
                <statement><p>You have the photointerrupter's output on the
                oscilloscope while the motor speeds up.  Which feature of the trace
                is telling you that the speed has changed?</p></statement>
                <choices randomize="yes">
                    <choice correct="yes">
                        <statement><p>The rate at which the pulses arrive — they
                        crowd together as the shaft speeds up.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>Correct.  The slots go past the sensor faster,
                        so the pulses arrive faster.  That rate is the only thing on
                        the trace that follows the shaft's speed, and it is what the
                        conversion to rpm is built on.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>The height of the pulses — they get
                        taller.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>The height of a pulse is set by the circuit the
                        sensor's output is connected to, not by how fast the wheel is
                        turning.  A wheel turning slowly and a wheel turning fast
                        produce pulses of the same height.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>The total number of pulses since the motor
                        started.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>That total tells you how far the shaft has
                        turned altogether, which is a useful thing to know but is not
                        a speed.  Speed is a count taken over a known interval of
                        time.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>How sharp the edges of the pulses
                        are.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>How quickly the output switches is a property of
                        the sensor, not of the shaft.  It is the same whether the
                        wheel is turning fast or slowly.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                </choices>
            </exercise>

            <exercise xml:id="rq-speed-slot-count">
                <statement><p>Two motors are turning at exactly the same speed.  One
                has a wheel with 20 slots and the other has a wheel with 12.  Which
                one produces more pulses per second?</p></statement>
                <choices randomize="yes">
                    <choice correct="yes">
                        <statement><p>The 20-slot wheel.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>Correct.  Both shafts complete the same number
                        of revolutions each second, and each revolution of the
                        20-slot wheel carries 20 slots past the sensor against the
                        other wheel's 12.  This is why the slot count has to appear
                        in the conversion: the same pulse rate means different speeds
                        on different wheels.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>The 12-slot wheel.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>Fewer slots means fewer interruptions of the
                        beam per revolution, so at the same shaft speed the 12-slot
                        wheel produces fewer pulses per second, not
                        more.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>They produce the same number, because the
                        shafts are turning at the same speed.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>The pulse rate depends on two things, not one:
                        how fast the shaft turns and how many slots each revolution
                        carries past the sensor.  Holding the first fixed and
                        changing the second changes the pulse rate.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>It cannot be worked out without knowing the
                        supply voltage of each motor.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>The supply voltage is what sets a motor's speed
                        in the first place, but here we are told the speeds are
                        already equal.  From that point on the pulse rate follows
                        from the shaft speed and the slot count
                        alone.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                </choices>
            </exercise>
        </reading-questions>

        <!--
        CANVAS QUIZ: "Before class - Measuring Motor Speed"
        (Due 1 hour before class; auto-graded multiple choice; low stakes.)

        Q1. A slotted wheel on a motor shaft has N slots.  How many pulses does the
            photointerrupter produce in one full revolution of the shaft?
            A) One.
            B) N.  <- CORRECT
            C) 2N.
            D) 60/N.

        Q2. You count PPS pulses in one second from a wheel with N slots.  Which
            expression gives the shaft speed in revolutions per minute?
            A) PPS x N / 60
            B) 60 x PPS / N  <- CORRECT
            C) PPS / (60 x N)
            D) 60 x N / PPS

        Q3. The wheel in your kit has 20 slots.  You count 30 pulses in one second.
            What is the shaft speed?
            A) 30 rpm
            B) 90 rpm  <- CORRECT  (60 x 30 / 20)
            C) 600 rpm
            D) 1800 rpm

        Q4. As the motor speeds up, what happens to the photointerrupter's output on
            an oscilloscope?
            A) The pulses get taller.
            B) The pulses crowd closer together.  <- CORRECT
            C) The pulses get shorter in height but stay equally spaced.
            D) Nothing changes until the motor reaches full speed.

        Q5. What does the photointerrupter's output need in order to work at all?
            A) A pull-up resistor on its output.  <- CORRECT
            B) A capacitor across its supply.
            C) A separate ground wire for each of its three pins.
            D) An amplifier between it and the microcontroller.
        -->
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <!-- DAY 12 — SKELETON.  The connecting prose between the figures,
         activities and slides below is written in the follow-up session
         (CHAPTER_PROCESS.md Step 4'), from the slides Petra has passed.
         Every subsection carries a "DELIVERY 2: prose." marker where a
         paragraph is expected.  Plan: plans/day12.md.  Gate 1 and its change
         list: reviews/day12-gate1.md. -->
    <section xml:id="sec-motors-day12">
        <title>Day 12 In-Class: Measuring the Motor's Speed</title>

        <introduction>
            <p>
                On Wednesday we read <c>TTmotor_ramp.c</c> one register at a time,
                and by the end of it we could set the motor to any speed we liked.
                Today we'll find out how fast it is actually turning.  We'll wire the
                photointerrupter and put its output on the oscilloscope, work out
                together how to turn a train of pulses into a number in revolutions
                per minute, and then build the rest of the Lab 6 system with that
                measurement in it.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <!-- Part 1 -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day12-driver-questions">
            <title>Part 1: Questions About TTmotor_ramp.c</title>

            <!-- DELIVERY 2: prose. -->

            <activity xml:id="act-day12-driver-questions">
                <title>What is still unclear about TTmotor_ramp.c</title>
                <introduction>
                    <p>Work on your own first, then compare with your table group.</p>
                </introduction>
                <task xml:id="task-day12-one-line">
                    <statement><p>Write down one line of <c>TTmotor_ramp.c</c> that
                    you can now explain, and one line that you cannot.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day12-table-questions">
                    <statement><p>With your table group, work through how the program
                    runs: what happens in <c>main()</c>, and which register each line
                    of the setup writes.  Put together the lines that nobody at the
                    table could explain.</p></statement>
                </task>
            </activity>
        </subsection>

        <!-- Part 2 -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day12-wire-and-scope">
            <title>Part 2: Wire the Sensor and Look at What It Says</title>

            <!-- DELIVERY 2: prose. -->

            <!-- P-6: the prediction comes BEFORE the resistor is wired, and
                 before fig-photointerrupter-states, which answers it.  Do not
                 reorder these three blocks.  The wiring figure deliberately
                 carries no pull-up VALUE and no OUT-to-PA15 arrow (P-15); the
                 omission is not an oversight. -->
            <activity xml:id="act-day12-predict-trace">
                <title>Predict the trace</title>
                <task xml:id="task-day12-predict-trace">
                    <statement><p>Suppose we power the photointerrupter, put the
                    oscilloscope on its output, and connect nothing else to that
                    output at all.  The motor is running, so slots are going past the
                    beam.  What would the trace look like, and why?</p></statement>
                </task>
            </activity>

            <figure xml:id="fig-day12-wiring">
                <caption>The circuit for this exercise, which is the one you built on
                Wednesday changed only by the sensor and one resistor.  The
                photointerrupter's power and ground go to the breadboard rails; its
                output goes to a row of its own, with a 10 kΩ resistor from that row
                up to 3.3 V (the blue arrow) and the oscilloscope on the same row.
                The output wire stops there — it does not go to the Nucleo today (the
                orange arrow), because a signal we have not looked at is a signal we
                cannot debug.  The TB6612, the motor and the three signal wires from
                PA5 (D13), PA6 (D12) and PA7 (D11) are unchanged from
                <xref ref="fig-tb6612-wiring-2"/>.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day12-Motors(3)/fig-day12-wiring.svg" width="88%"/>
            </figure>
            <slide xml:id="sl-day12-wiring" ref="fig-day12-wiring" stack="yes">
    <ul>
        <li><clr c="blue">Blue arrow:</clr> a 10 kΩ resistor from the sensor's output up to 3.3 V.</li>
        <li><clr c="orange">Orange arrow:</clr> the output wire stops at the breadboard. It does not go into the Nucleo today.</li>
        <li>Everything else on the board is what you wired on Wednesday.</li>
    </ul>
    <note>≈ 2 min to read the picture, then the activity. Her slide 6. Say first that we are back to the bare circuit — take the scope and the multimeter off the PWM node from Wednesday before anything else. The signal wire is orange in this drawing and white on the part in your kit; say so or somebody will look for an orange wire. Do NOT say what the pull-up is for yet — the prediction has just been made and fig-photointerrupter-states is the reveal.</note>
</slide>

            <activity xml:id="act-day12-wire-and-scope">
                <title>Wire the sensor, and watch it while the motor ramps</title>
                <task xml:id="task-day12-wire">
                    <statement><p>Wire the photointerrupter's power and ground, and
                    run its output to a row of its own on the breadboard.  Put the
                    10 kΩ resistor between that row and 3.3 V.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day12-scope">
                    <statement><p>Put the oscilloscope on that row with Waveforms, and
                    turn the wheel slowly by hand.  You are looking for a trace that
                    toggles between 0 V and 3.3 V once for every slot that goes
                    past.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day12-ramp">
                    <statement><p>Run <c>TTmotor_ramp.c</c> and watch the trace as the
                    motor speeds up and slows down again.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day12-measure">
                    <statement><p>At the fastest part of the ramp, measure how many
                    pulses arrive per second.  Write the number down — we'll use it
                    twice more today.</p></statement>
                </task>
            </activity>

            <table xml:id="table-day12-diagnostics">
                <title>What the trace is telling you</title>
                <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="major" left="minor" right="minor">
                    <col width="42%"/>
                    <col width="58%"/>
                    <row header="yes" bottom="major">
                        <cell>What you see</cell>
                        <cell>Where to look</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row bottom="minor">
                        <cell>Noise wandering around somewhere between the rails</cell>
                        <cell>Nothing is pulling the line up, so the pin is floating.  Check the resistor and the rail it goes to.</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row bottom="minor">
                        <cell>A flat line at 3.3 V that never moves</cell>
                        <cell>The beam is never being blocked, or the LED side has no power.  Check that the rim is actually in the gap, and check power and ground.</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row bottom="minor">
                        <cell>A flat line at 0 V</cell>
                        <cell>Power and ground are most likely swapped at the sensor.</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell>A clean square wave that does not change when the motor speeds up</cell>
                        <cell>The probe is on the wrong node.  Follow it back to the row the output wire lands in.</cell>
                    </row>
                </tabular>
            </table>
            <slide xml:id="sl-day12-diagnostics" ref="table-day12-diagnostics">
    <caption>Four traces and where each one sends you. The first row is the one to expect if the pull-up is missing.</caption>
    <note>≈ 1 min, and leave it up while they wire. This is content, not classroom management: each row is a consequence of the circuit in fig-photointerrupter-states, not advice about who to ask.</note>
</slide>

            <figure xml:id="fig-photointerrupter-states">
                <caption>Why the sensor's output needs a pull-up resistor.  The
                phototransistor is a switch to ground and nothing more: when a slot
                lets the beam through it conducts and pulls OUT down to 0 V, and when
                a spoke blocks the beam it stops conducting and lets go of the line
                altogether.  It has no way to drive OUT up.  The 10 kΩ resistor is
                what does that, which means whatever the resistor is connected to is
                what sets the HIGH level — here 3.3 V, which is what the
                STM32C031C6's pins expect.  With no resistor there, nothing holds the
                line up when the transistor lets go, and the pin is left floating.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day12-Motors(3)/fig-photointerrupter-states.svg" width="96%"/>
            </figure>
            <slide xml:id="sl-day12-states" ref="fig-photointerrupter-states">
    <caption>The phototransistor can only pull OUT down. Whatever the resistor is tied to is what sets the HIGH level.</caption>
    <note>≈ 3 min, and this is the reveal for the prediction. Draw out the consequence rather than stating it: the sensor cannot put a voltage on the line, it can only take one away, so the HIGH level is ours to choose. That is why the pull-up goes to 3.3 V. This is the same behavior as the open-drain outputs of Day 10, on a different kind of transistor — say that, and say there is no OTYPER bit on the sensor, or somebody will go looking for one.</note>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day12-pullup-value">
    <ul>
        <li>Why a resistor at all: the phototransistor can pull OUT down but cannot drive it up, so something has to hold the line up when it lets go.</li>
        <li>Why it goes to 3.3 V: the resistor's rail is what sets the HIGH level, and 3.3 V is what the STM32C031C6's pins expect.</li>
        <li>Why about 10 kΩ: much smaller wastes current and makes the transistor work harder to pull the line down; much larger takes too long to bring the line back up against the stray capacitance of the wiring, and picks up more noise.</li>
        <li>In Lab 6 you can use this resistor, or enable the pin's own internal pull-up with <c>PUPDR</c> the way you did on Day 3. Today it is the external one, because we are putting a scope probe on that node.</li>
    </ul>
    <note>≈ 2 min. The third bullet is the one they have not had before — Day 3 and Day 10 both gave them pull-ups without a value argument. Do not turn it into a calculation; it is a trade-off with a wide middle, which is why 10 kΩ is the answer to a lot of these questions.</note>
</slide>

            <figure xml:id="fig-day12-cabled-sensor">
                <caption>The photointerrupter as some kits have it, on a length of
                four-core cable rather than on a small board.  It is the same part
                and the same three connections, but the wires are identified by color
                instead of by a silkscreen: brown is power, blue is ground, and black
                is the output.  The pink wire drives the LED separately and is not
                needed here.  The same list is in the Lab 6 handout.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day12-Motors(3)/fig-day12-cabled-sensor.jpg" width="62%"/>
            </figure>
            <slide xml:id="sl-day12-cabled-sensor" ref="fig-day12-cabled-sensor" stack="yes">
    <ul>
        <li>Brown — power.</li>
        <li>Blue — ground.</li>
        <li>Black — the output, and the one that gets the pull-up.</li>
        <li>Pink — the LED, driven separately. We do not need it.</li>
    </ul>
    <note>≈ 1 min, and only if somebody has this variant — her slide 7 exists because some kits have it and some do not. If nobody in the room is holding one, skip it and say the colors are in the lab handout.</note>
</slide>

            <instructor xml:id="inst-day12-wire-and-scope">
                <p><term>The prediction.</term>  With nothing connected to the output
                but the oscilloscope, the trace is not a square wave and it is not a
                flat line either — it wanders, and it may well look like it is
                responding to the motor, because a floating input picks up whatever
                is nearby.  The phototransistor pulls the line to 0 V when the beam
                reaches it, and then simply stops conducting when the beam is blocked.
                Nothing brings the line back up, so between pulses the node is
                connected to nothing at all.</p>
                <p><term>What they should have on the screen.</term>  A square wave
                between 0 V and 3.3 V, one cycle per slot, whose frequency climbs and
                falls with the ramp.  Turning the wheel by hand is the check worth
                insisting on before the motor is started, because it is the only test
                where the student controls the input.</p>
                <p><term>The number.</term>  Twenty slots at the top of the ramp gives
                a pulse rate in the tens of hertz — the exact figure depends on how
                fast their motor is turning, and it is theirs to measure rather than
                ours to state.  What matters is that they write it down: Part 3 turns
                it into rpm and Part 4 uses it to decide how the pulses get
                counted.</p>
            </instructor>
        </subsection>

        <!-- Part 3 -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day12-pulses-to-rpm">
            <title>Part 3: From a Pulse Train to a Number</title>

            <!-- DELIVERY 2: prose. -->

            <!-- P-6: task-day12-detect is deliberately left open here.  Part 4 is
                 where it is answered, and nothing in this subsection or in
                 sl-day12-rpm may answer it early. -->
            <activity xml:id="act-day12-pulses-to-rpm">
                <title>Turning the pulses into a speed</title>
                <introduction>
                    <p>On paper, with your table group.</p>
                </introduction>
                <task xml:id="task-day12-count-slots">
                    <statement><p>Count the slots on your own wheel.  How many pulses
                    does one full revolution of the shaft produce?</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day12-detect">
                    <statement><p>How could the STM32C031C6 notice each pulse as it
                    arrives?  Describe every way you can think of, and say what each
                    one would cost the rest of the program.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day12-convert">
                    <statement><p>Take the pulse rate you measured at the top of the
                    ramp and convert it to revolutions per minute, using your own slot
                    count.  Is the answer plausible for the motor you just
                    watched?</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day12-direction">
                    <statement><p>Can you tell which way the shaft is turning from
                    this signal alone?  If you can, say how; if you cannot, say what
                    would have to be different about the sensor.</p></statement>
                </task>
            </activity>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day12-rpm" room="yes">
    <p>Regardless of the technology — mechanical, optical or magnetic — a sensor like this one gives us a pulse train, and we have to turn that pulse train into position or velocity information.</p>
    <image source="images/Day12-Motors(3)/rpm-formula.svg" width="24%"/>
    <ul>
        <li><term>PPS</term> is the count of pulses in one second, and <m>N</m> is the number of slots you counted. Divide by <m>N</m> to get revolutions, multiply by 60 to get a rate per minute.</li>
        <li>Count <em>one edge per slot</em> — the rising edge or the falling edge, not both. Counting both doubles the pulse count, and then the formula needs <m>2N</m> in place of <m>N</m>.</li>
    </ul>
    <note>≈ 3 min. The formula is from the reading, so this is not new — what is new is that both numbers going into it are theirs: the slot count they just counted, and the rate they measured in Part 2. Have a table read out both and do it on the board. The one-edge rule is the finding worth spending a minute on: they have FTSR1 and RTSR1 from Day 9 and triggering on both is a natural thing to try, and it reports twice the true speed with nothing on the screen to say why.</note>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day12-decoding">
    <ul>
        <li>The same count read as an angle: each pulse is <m>\Delta\theta = 2\pi / N</m> radians of shaft rotation, or <m>360/N</m> degrees, or <m>1/N</m> of a revolution.</li>
        <li>So <m>\text{pulse count} \times \Delta\theta</m> is how far the shaft has turned since we started counting — its position, relative to wherever it was then.</li>
        <li>And <m>(\text{pulse count} / \text{time}) \times \Delta\theta</m> is its average speed over that interval, which is the rpm we just worked out in different units.</li>
        <li>Note the word <em>average</em>: this measurement cannot tell us anything about what happened inside the interval we counted over.</li>
    </ul>
    <note>≈ 3 min. Her slide 21. The point is that one count answers two different questions depending on what we divide by, and that position is relative — the sensor has no idea where the shaft started. The last bullet is the seam into the stretch on the measurement's own resolution.</note>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day12-quadrature">
    <ul>
        <li>Nothing in this signal carries direction. Every slot looks like every other slot, whichever way the wheel turns.</li>
        <li>A <term>quadrature encoder</term> uses two sensors a quarter of a slot apart, so the two pulse trains are offset. Which one leads tells you the direction, and an up-down counter then gives position and speed together.</li>
        <li>Some sensors do all of this decoding in hardware and hand you the answer over a bus — over I2C, for instance, which you already have working.</li>
    </ul>
    <note>≈ 2 min, and this is the first thing to cut if Part 2 ran long: it survives as the second stretch and in the chapter's reference material. If it is cut, still say the first bullet out loud, because Lab 6 wants a negative rpm and the students have to know the sign is not coming from the sensor.</note>
</slide>

            <instructor xml:id="inst-day12-pulses-to-rpm">
                <p><term>The slot count.</term>  Twenty, on the wheels in the kit,
                which is the number the expression in Lab 6 uses.  Having them count
                it rather than take it from the handout is worth the thirty seconds:
                the slot count is the only quantity in the conversion that comes from
                the hardware, and a student who swaps a wheel later has to know
                that.</p>
                <p><term>Detecting the pulses.</term>  Leave this open — Part 4 is
                where it gets settled.  What to collect from the tables now, without
                ruling on any of it: read the pin in the main loop; use a GPIO
                interrupt on a falling edge, as on Day 9; use a timer's input capture,
                which this course has not taught; and count in hardware, which the
                STM32C031C6's TIM3 can do but which is beyond what Lab 6 needs.  If a
                table says <q>an interrupt, like the button on Day 9</q>, that is the
                right answer and it still does not get confirmed here.</p>
                <p><term>The conversion.</term>  Whatever they measured, divided by
                their slot count, times 60.  The check that matters is plausibility
                rather than accuracy: a three-digit rpm is right for this motor, and
                an answer in the thousands means the slot count went in the wrong
                place.</p>
                <p><term>Direction.</term>  No.  One sensor and identical slots give a
                signal that is the same in both directions, so the sign on the display
                in Lab 6 cannot come from the measurement — it has to come from what
                the program most recently commanded.  That is worth saying plainly,
                because it means the display is trusting the motor to be doing what it
                was told, and a stalled or back-driven motor would be shown with the
                wrong sign.  Getting direction out of the measurement itself needs a
                second sensor, offset from the first.</p>
            </instructor>
        </subsection>

        <!-- Part 4 -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day12-main-loop">
            <title>Part 4: A Loop That Does Three Things at Three Rates</title>

            <!-- DELIVERY 2: prose. -->

            <slide xml:id="sl-day12-three-rates">
    <ul>
        <li>By the end of Lab 6 your program has three jobs, and they run at three different rates: sample the potentiometer 100 times a second, notice every pulse from the sensor as it arrives, and put a new number on the display.</li>
        <li><c>delay_ms()</c> can only do one of them. While it is counting out its milliseconds, the program is doing nothing else at all — which is the same problem we watched a polled button counter have on Day 9.</li>
        <li>In Lab 6 you are told not to use <c>delay_ms()</c> for timing at all, and to use <c>milliseconds()</c> instead.</li>
    </ul>
    <note>≈ 1 min. Do not explain milliseconds() yet — the next slide is the code. The Day 9 callback is the one to lean on: they have already seen a loop miss an event because it was busy waiting.</note>
</slide>

            <!-- B-6: copied verbatim from assets/starters/sysinit.c, which is in
                 every project the course ships.  SysTickInit is registered in
                 scripts/check_starters.py against this listing. -->
            <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
static uint32_t currentMilliseconds = 0;

void SysTick_Handler(void) {
	currentMilliseconds++;
}

inline uint32_t milliseconds(void) {
	return currentMilliseconds;
}
]]></code></program>

            <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
void SysTickInit(void) {

	// Assumes 12MHz clock...could be better
	SysTick->LOAD = 12000 - 1;

	SysTick->CTRL |= SysTick_CTRL_CLKSOURCE_Msk;  // Select the processor clock
	SysTick->CTRL |= SysTick_CTRL_TICKINT_Msk;    // Enable the interrupt
	SysTick->CTRL |= SysTick_CTRL_ENABLE_Msk;     // Turn it on.

}
]]></code></program>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day12-milliseconds">
    <p>This has been in every project you have built this term, in <c>sysinit.c</c>, and we have never opened it.</p>
    <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
static uint32_t currentMilliseconds = 0;

void SysTick_Handler(void) {          // runs once every millisecond
	currentMilliseconds++;
}

inline uint32_t milliseconds(void) {  // milliseconds since the board came up
	return currentMilliseconds;
}

void SysTickInit(void) {
	SysTick->LOAD = 12000 - 1;                    // 12 MHz / 12000 = 1 kHz
	SysTick->CTRL |= SysTick_CTRL_CLKSOURCE_Msk;  // Select the processor clock
	SysTick->CTRL |= SysTick_CTRL_TICKINT_Msk;    // Enable the interrupt
	SysTick->CTRL |= SysTick_CTRL_ENABLE_Msk;     // Turn it on.
}
]]></code></program>
    <caption>SysTick counts down from 12000 at 12 MHz, so it interrupts once a millisecond and the handler does one thing.</caption>
    <note>≈ 3 min. Three things to draw out. It is a timer interrupt, exactly like the one they set up on Day 8, so nothing here is new machinery. delay_ms() has always been built on it — this is what it has been reading all term. And milliseconds() does not wait for anything: it hands back a number and returns, so asking the time costs nothing. The listing here is abridged from sysinit.c only by its comments; the code is the file's.</note>
</slide>

            <!-- P-9: the naive version.  This loop is TEACHING code, deliberately
                 broken, and it is not any driver in assets/starters. Do not
                 register it in check_starters.py and do not hand it out. -->
            <slide xml:id="sl-day12-naive-loop" room="yes">
    <p>Here is a first attempt at the loop. The potentiometer is sampled at 100 Hz, which is what Lab 6 asks for, and the sensor's pin is read in the same beat.</p>
    <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
#define SENSOR_PIN  (1U<<15)          // the photointerrupter, on PA15 (D7)

    uint32_t last_sample = 0;
    uint8_t  now, before = 1;
    uint16_t pulses = 0;

    while (1) {
        if (milliseconds() - last_sample >= 10) {          // every 10 ms
            last_sample = milliseconds();

            start_conversion();                            // the potentiometer
            speed = adc_read();

            now = (GPIOA->IDR & SENSOR_PIN) ? 1 : 0;       // the sensor's output
            if (before == 1 && now == 0)                   // a falling edge?
                pulses++;
            before = now;
        }
    }
]]></code></program>
    <ul>
        <li>Your motor at the top of the ramp gives the pulse rate you measured in Part 2. After one second of this, what does <c>pulses</c> hold?</li>
    </ul>
    <note>≈ 2 min, predict before revealing. The answer is: fewer than it should, and not by a fixed amount. The loop looks at the pin 100 times a second, and each look tells it only what the pin is doing at that instant — a slot that arrives and leaves between two looks is never seen at all. Draw the pulse train on the board and put 10 ms tick marks under it. The precise statement is worth getting right: the read has to happen more often than the SHORTER of the pulse's HIGH time and its LOW time, and that is set by how wide the slots are compared to the spokes, not by the pulse rate on its own.</note>
</slide>

            <activity xml:id="act-day12-poll-or-interrupt">
                <title>Poll or interrupt</title>
                <task xml:id="task-day12-commit">
                    <statement><p>Work on your own for a moment and write down an
                    answer, then we'll collect them.  You measured a pulse rate at the
                    top of the ramp in Part 2.  In the program you are about to write
                    for Lab 6, will you read the sensor's pin in the main loop, or
                    take an interrupt on it?  Give the reason that decided it for
                    you.</p></statement>
                </task>
            </activity>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day12-two-answers">
    <ul>
        <li><term>Read the pin every pass.</term> Move the read out of the 10 ms beat so the loop looks at it as fast as it can go, and use <c>milliseconds()</c> only to decide when to sample the potentiometer and when to close the one-second counting window. This works, and it costs a promise: nothing anywhere in the loop may ever block, for as long as the program lives.</li>
        <li><term>Take a falling-edge interrupt on PA15.</term> This is what we set up on Day 9 for the button on PB4, with 15 in place of 4 — and PA15 is on the same handler you already wrote, <c>EXTI4_15_IRQHandler</c>. The port selection is in <c>EXTI_EXTICR4</c>, which the Reference Manual gives in section 12.5.9.</li>
        <li>The second one is the one this course goes with, and the reason is not the arithmetic. The arithmetic depends on how fast this particular motor turns and how wide these particular slots are. The interrupt does not: the pulse is counted whatever else the loop is doing, at any speed.</li>
    </ul>
    <note>≈ 3 min. Take three or four answers from the room BEFORE this slide goes up — the commit is the only place all day where a student makes this decision themselves. Describe the polled answer honestly rather than dismissing it: it is what Lab 6's text allows, and at these rates it works. What retires it is not that it fails here but that whether it fails depends on numbers nobody measured.</note>
</slide>

            <instructor xml:id="inst-day12-poll-or-interrupt">
                <p><term>What the naive loop reports.</term>  Fewer pulses than went
                past, and an unpredictable number of them.  The loop looks at the pin
                once every 10 ms and learns only what the pin is doing at that
                instant.  A slot whose whole pulse falls between two looks is never
                seen, and because the pulse train and the 10 ms beat are not
                synchronized the count drifts around rather than being wrong by a
                fixed factor.</p>
                <p><term>The condition, stated properly.</term>  A polled read catches
                every pulse only if the gap between reads is shorter than the shorter
                of the pulse's HIGH time and its LOW time.  Those two are set by how
                wide the slots are compared to the metal between them, and by the
                shaft speed — so on a wheel with slots and spokes of about equal width,
                a pulse rate of <m>f</m> gives a HIGH time of about <m>1/(2f)</m>.  It
                is worth resisting the shorter version of this argument, that a
                100 Hz loop cannot see a 60 Hz signal, because it is not quite true
                and a student who takes it away will apply it wrongly.</p>
                <p><term>Both answers are correct, and one of them is better for a
                reason that is not about this motor.</term>  A free-running loop that
                reads the pin every pass catches these pulses comfortably, and it is
                what Lab 6's own wording allows.  What it costs is a standing
                constraint on every line added to that loop afterwards.  The interrupt
                costs a handler and a <c>volatile</c> counter and then stops being
                anybody's problem — and it is the one the chapter's objectives commit
                to.</p>
                <p><term>What they already have for it.</term>  The five moves are
                Day 9's, unchanged: select port A on the line in
                <c>EXTI_EXTICR4</c>, enable the falling-edge trigger in
                <c>EXTI_FTSR1</c>, unmask the line in <c>EXTI_IMR1</c>, enable the
                line in the NVIC, and clear the pending bit in <c>EXTI_FPR1</c> inside
                the handler.  The line number is the bit number in all three EXTI
                registers, so every 4 in <c>counterResetButtonInt.c</c> becomes a 15.
                The one thing that is genuinely different is the port: <c>EXTI_PA</c>
                rather than <c>EXTI_PB</c>, and <c>EXTI->EXTICR[3]</c> rather than
                <c>EXTI->EXTICR[1]</c>.</p>
            </instructor>
        </subsection>

        <!-- Part 5 -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day12-whole-build">
            <title>Part 5: The Whole Build</title>

            <!-- DELIVERY 2: prose. -->

            <activity xml:id="act-day12-find-the-pin">
                <title>Which pin is behind D7</title>
                <introduction>
                    <p>The Nucleo's headers are labeled with Arduino names, and the
                    reference manual and the datasheet are not.  The user manual for
                    the board is what translates between them: UM2953, Table 11,
                    <em>ARDUINO connector pinout</em>, on page 20.</p>
                </introduction>
                <task xml:id="task-day12-d7">
                    <statement><p>The sensor's output goes to header pin <c>D7</c>.
                    Find the row for <c>D7</c> in that table and write down which
                    STM32C031C6 pin it is.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day12-d7-check">
                    <statement><p>Table 12 of the same manual lists the board's I/O
                    assignment the other way round, by port pin.  Find your answer
                    there as well, and check that the two tables
                    agree.</p></statement>
                </task>
            </activity>
            <instructor xml:id="inst-day12-find-the-pin">
                <p><c>D7</c> is <c>PA15</c>.  UM2953 Table 11 gives it on connector
                CN9, pin 8: <em>D7 · ARD_D7 · PA15 · I/O</em>.  Table 12 has the same
                fact from the other side, at pin 37: <em>PA15 · ARD_D7</em>.</p>
                <p>Two things worth naming while the table is open.  <c>PA15</c> has
                no alternate function in the <em>Function</em> column, only
                <q>I/O</q> — unlike D11 beside it, which carries
                <c>TIM_14_CH1</c> and is why the PWM comes out there.  And the
                lookup is a two-minute job that saves a wrong guess in the lab, which
                is the whole reason for doing it in the room rather than printing the
                answer.</p>
            </instructor>

            <figure xml:id="fig-day12-lab6-build">
                <caption>The complete Lab 6 system.  The 9 V adapter feeds the
                regulator board on the right, whose 5 V output powers the motor
                through the TB6612 and nothing else.  The Nucleo supplies 3.3 V for
                the logic, and every part on the board shares one ground.  Working
                from the left: the seven-segment display on the I2C bus, the
                potentiometer feeding A0, the TB6612 with the motor on its A channel,
                and the photointerrupter at the bottom left with its output going to
                D7.  Every one of those pieces has appeared in this chapter or an
                earlier one; what is new is that they are all on the board at
                once.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day12-Motors(3)/fig-day12-lab6-build.svg" width="94%"/>
            </figure>
            <slide xml:id="sl-day12-lab6-build" ref="fig-day12-lab6-build">
    <caption>Everything on this board you have wired before, one piece at a time. Lab 6 is the first time they are all on it together.</caption>
    <note>≈ 2 min. Her slide 10. The picture's job is the block layout — do NOT ask them to read pin names off it, because the Nucleo silkscreen in this image is far too small to project. Every pin name they need is on the build-order slide as text. Point at the four blocks and the two supplies, and move on.</note>
</slide>

            <table xml:id="table-day12-build-order">
                <title>The order to build in, and how you know each stage works</title>
                <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="major" left="minor" right="minor">
                    <col width="46%"/>
                    <col width="54%"/>
                    <row header="yes" bottom="major">
                        <cell>Stage</cell>
                        <cell>It is working when</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row bottom="minor">
                        <cell>The <c>tb6612.c</c> driver alone, with no motor connected — PA5 (D13), PA6 (D12) and PA7 (D11)</cell>
                        <cell>Waveforms shows a 1.6 kHz square wave on PA7 whose duty cycle follows the speed you asked for, and IN1 and IN2 follow the mode</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row bottom="minor">
                        <cell>The potentiometer on A0 (PA0)</cell>
                        <cell>The value you read from the ADC sweeps from one end of its range to the other as you turn the knob</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row bottom="minor">
                        <cell>The regulator board, with nothing else connected to it</cell>
                        <cell>9 V on its input side and 5 V on its output side, on the multimeter</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row bottom="minor">
                        <cell>The motor, through the TB6612</cell>
                        <cell>The knob drives it both ways and stops it in the middle</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row bottom="minor">
                        <cell>The photointerrupter on D7 (PA15)</cell>
                        <cell>The trace toggles once per slot, as it did in Part 2</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row bottom="minor">
                        <cell>The rpm, printed to your screen</cell>
                        <cell>The number tracks the motor and is plausible for what you can see the shaft doing</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell>The rpm on the seven-segment display</cell>
                        <cell>The same number appears there, with a minus sign when the motor is running in reverse</cell>
                    </row>
                </tabular>
            </table>
            <slide xml:id="sl-day12-build-order" ref="table-day12-build-order">
    <caption>Test each stage before adding the next one. The last two are firmware only, and the display is deliberately last.</caption>
    <note>≈ 2 min. This is Lab 6's own order for the hardware and her slide 10's order for the firmware. Two things to say out loud: the driver is tested with no motor connected, which is the habit worth keeping; and getting the rpm onto the screen is a milestone that does not depend on the display working, so nobody spends the evening stuck on I2C with nothing to show.</note>
</slide>

            <table xml:id="table-day12-deadband">
                <title>The potentiometer, mapped to a mode and a speed</title>
                <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="major" left="minor" right="minor">
                    <col width="22%"/>
                    <col width="20%"/>
                    <col width="24%"/>
                    <col width="34%"/>
                    <row header="yes" bottom="major">
                        <cell>Knob</cell>
                        <cell>Voltage on A0</cell>
                        <cell>ADC count, of 4095</cell>
                        <cell>What the motor does</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row bottom="minor">
                        <cell>Fully counter-clockwise</cell>
                        <cell>0 V</cell>
                        <cell>0</cell>
                        <cell>Full speed counter-clockwise</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row bottom="minor">
                        <cell>Centered</cell>
                        <cell>1.65 V</cell>
                        <cell>about 2048</cell>
                        <cell>Stopped, and stopped for a band of counts either side of 2048 rather than at one count</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell>Fully clockwise</cell>
                        <cell>3.3 V</cell>
                        <cell>4095</cell>
                        <cell>Full speed clockwise</cell>
                    </row>
                </tabular>
            </table>
            <slide xml:id="sl-day12-deadband" ref="table-day12-deadband">
    <caption>The dead band is the range of counts around the center where the motor stays stopped. Without it the motor dithers between forward and reverse.</caption>
    <note>≈ 2 min. Work one number on the board: 12 bits gives 4096 counts across 3.3 V, so a count is about 0.8 mV and the center is about 2048. Then ask how wide the band should be — there is no right answer, and the reasoning is what matters: wide enough that a hand resting on the knob does not flip the direction, narrow enough that the useful range of the knob is not eaten up. A hundred counts either side is about 80 mV and is a reasonable place to start. The mapping from the two remaining ranges onto 0-1249 of PWM is theirs to write.</note>
</slide>

            <p>
                Three things about this circuit are worth being careful with, because
                they are the ones that damage hardware rather than simply not
                working.  The potentiometer's two outer legs go to <term>3.3 V and
                ground</term>, and never to the 5 V rail: its wiper feeds PA0, which
                is an analog input on a 3.3 V part.  The regulator's 5 V output is
                motor power only, and goes to the TB6612's motor supply and nowhere
                else — putting it on the breadboard's power rail puts it on the
                Nucleo.  And the 9 V adapter, the regulator and the Nucleo now all
                share one ground, which every part on the board has to be connected
                to; a circuit whose grounds are not common does not work, and the
                symptoms look like everything else.
            </p>
            <slide xml:id="sl-day12-hazards">
    <ul>
        <li>The potentiometer's outer legs go to <term>3.3 V and ground</term>. Its wiper feeds PA0, which is an analog input on a 3.3 V part.</li>
        <li>The regulator's <term>5 V is motor power only</term>. It goes to the TB6612's motor supply and nowhere else — on the breadboard's power rail it reaches the Nucleo.</li>
        <li>The 9 V adapter, the regulator and the Nucleo <term>share one ground</term>, and so does every part on the board.</li>
        <li>The regulator gets hot with the motor running. If something smells hot or is hot to the touch, disconnect the power and check the wiring before anything else.</li>
    </ul>
    <note>≈ 1 min, and say it once rather than repeating it during the build. The first bullet is the one that is easy to get wrong: Lab 6's own schematic draws the pot on the 5 V net, which is a leftover, and the lab text describes a 3.3 V pot.</note>
</slide>
        </subsection>

        <!-- Part 6 -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day12-build">
            <title>Part 6: Build</title>

            <!-- DELIVERY 2: prose. -->

            <activity xml:id="act-day12-main-loop-sketch">
                <title>Sketch the main loop before you write it</title>
                <introduction>
                    <p>On paper, before the keyboard.  Your program has to do three
                    things at three different rates.</p>
                </introduction>
                <task xml:id="task-day12-sketch-rates">
                    <statement><p>Write down what the loop does on <em>every</em>
                    pass, what it does every 10 ms, and what it does once a second.
                    Put beside each one what decides that it is time.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day12-sketch-window">
                    <statement><p>The rpm comes from counting pulses over one second.
                    Where in your sketch does the count get read, and where does it
                    get set back to zero?  What happens to a pulse that arrives
                    between those two moments?</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day12-sketch-shared">
                    <statement><p>If the count is incremented in an interrupt handler
                    and read in the main loop, what does its declaration need that an
                    ordinary variable does not, and why?</p></statement>
                </task>
            </activity>
            <instructor xml:id="inst-day12-main-loop-sketch">
                <p><term>The three rates.</term>  Every pass: nothing, if the pulses
                are being counted by the interrupt — which is the point of using one.
                Every 10 ms, decided by <c>milliseconds()</c>: read the ADC, map it to
                a mode and a PWM value, and call the driver.  Once a second, decided
                by <c>milliseconds()</c> again: take the pulse count, convert it to
                rpm, send it to the display, and start the next window.</p>
                <p><term>The window.</term>  The count is read and zeroed at the same
                moment, once a second.  A pulse that arrives between the read and the
                zeroing is lost, which on a 20-slot wheel is at most one pulse in a
                second and therefore at most a few rpm — small, but worth having
                noticed rather than discovered.  Reading and zeroing in one step, or
                subtracting the value read rather than assigning zero, both avoid
                it.</p>
                <p><term>The declaration.</term>  <c>volatile</c>, exactly as on
                Day 9.  Without it the compiler is entitled to assume that nothing
                changes the counter between two reads in the main loop, because
                nothing in the main loop does — so it may keep the value in a register
                and never look at memory again.  The handler writes to memory, the
                loop reads its stale register copy, and the display stops
                moving.</p>
                <p><term>A worked shape</term>, not the only one, and not code to hand
                out:</p>
                <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
volatile uint16_t pulses = 0;             // written by the ISR, read by main()

int main(void) {
    uint32_t last_sample = 0, last_second = 0;

    // ... init the driver, the ADC, the display and the EXTI line ...

    while (1) {
        if (milliseconds() - last_sample >= 10) {      // 100 Hz
            last_sample += 10;
            speed = adc_read();
            // map speed to a mode and a PWM value, then call the driver
        }
        if (milliseconds() - last_second >= 1000) {    // once a second
            last_second += 1000;
            count  = pulses;  pulses = 0;              // read and clear together
            rpm    = 60 * count / SLOTS;
            // show rpm, with the sign taken from the mode we commanded
        }
    }
    return 1;
}
]]></code></program>
                <p>Two details in that shape are worth pointing at if a group asks.
                Adding the interval to <c>last_sample</c> rather than assigning
                <c>milliseconds()</c> to it keeps the average rate exact even when a
                pass runs late.  And the sign on the rpm comes from the mode the
                program commanded, because the pulse train has no direction in it —
                which is the answer to the fourth question in Part 3.</p>
            </instructor>

            <activity xml:id="act-day12-stretch">
                <title>Going further</title>
                <introduction>
                    <p>These are optional, for when the rpm is on your display.</p>
                </introduction>
                <task xml:id="task-day12-stretch-sign">
                    <statement><p>The sign on the displayed rpm comes from what your
                    program commanded, not from what it measured.  Describe a
                    situation in which the motor is not doing what it was told, and
                    say what the display would show.  What would have to change for
                    the measurement itself to carry the sign?</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day12-stretch-quadrature">
                    <statement><p>A quadrature encoder puts a second sensor a quarter
                    of a slot away from the first.  Sketch the two output waveforms
                    for a wheel turning clockwise, then for the same wheel turning
                    counter-clockwise, and say precisely what a program would have to
                    look at to tell them apart.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day12-stretch-resolution">
                    <statement><p>Counting whole pulses in a one-second window
                    quantizes the answer: work out how many rpm one pulse of miscount
                    is worth on your wheel, and what fraction of the reading that is
                    at a slow speed.  Then take three ways of doing better — count for
                    longer, count both edges, or time the interval between two pulses
                    instead of counting — and say what each one costs and where each
                    one wins.</p></statement>
                </task>
            </activity>
            <instructor xml:id="inst-day12-stretch">
                <p><term>The sign.</term>  A motor that is stalled against a load, or
                one being turned by hand, or one still coasting after the command
                changed, all produce pulses that the program will label with whatever
                it last commanded.  A stalled motor commanded forward shows a positive
                rpm near zero; a shaft turned backwards by hand while the program
                commands forward shows a positive rpm that is entirely wrong in sign.
                Getting the sign from the measurement needs a second sensor offset
                from the first, which is the quadrature encoder in the next
                task.</p>
                <p><term>Quadrature.</term>  Two trains of the same frequency, one a
                quarter of a cycle ahead of the other.  Turning the other way swaps
                which one leads.  What a program looks at is the state of one channel
                at the moment the other one changes: on a rising edge of A, channel B
                is LOW in one direction and HIGH in the other.  That is a two-bit
                state machine, and it also doubles or quadruples the resolution,
                because there are now four distinguishable edges per slot instead of
                one.</p>
                <p><term>Resolution.</term>  One pulse in a one-second window is
                <m>60/N</m> rpm, which on a 20-slot wheel is <m>3</m> rpm.  At 30 rpm
                the reading is 10 pulses, so one pulse is 10% of it; at 180 rpm it is
                60 pulses and one pulse is under 2%.  The three remedies:</p>
                <p><term>Count for longer.</term>  A two-second window halves the
                quantization and doubles how long the display takes to notice a
                change.  That trade is the whole of it.</p>
                <p><term>Count both edges.</term>  Twice the count for the same
                rotation, so half the quantization — and the conversion has to change
                with it, from <m>N</m> to <m>2N</m>.  A group that makes this change
                and forgets the second half reports exactly twice the true speed, and
                nothing on the display says so.</p>
                <p><term>Time the interval instead.</term>  Measure the milliseconds
                between two consecutive pulses and invert.  This inverts the trade as
                well: at 3 rpm the interval is about a second and a 1 ms tick resolves
                it to about a tenth of a percent, where counting sees a single pulse
                in its whole window; at 180 rpm the interval is about 17 ms and the
                same 1 ms tick gives about 6%, which is worse than counting's 2%.  So
                counting wins at speed and interval-timing wins at rest, and a real
                instrument does both and crosses over.</p>
            </instructor>
        </subsection>
    </section>

